04:48.789 --> 04:50.761
While you're pulling that up, I'm just— I was going to say this in a

04:50.777 --> 04:54.207
minute, but I, I'm going to jump backwards. I skipped item 1A,

04:54.255 --> 04:57.702
and I did not mention why, but I knew why, and I forgot to mention

04:58.202 --> 05:01.758
it. Uh, I'm switching the order on those, but then David Stroud called in.

05:02.575 --> 05:05.108
Uh, he was not going to be here, so he wanted to move that item

05:05.156 --> 05:09.613
to 10:20. I do want to have a short discussion

05:10.113 --> 05:12.347
discussion, if there's anything that we can at least give direction. So I'll come right

05:12.847 --> 05:15.906
back to that for the council. Okay,

05:16.051 --> 05:19.195
now that we're all set up with this, Tony is going to take over to

05:19.695 --> 05:23.031
first talk about the, the revenue and the expense side of things, and then I'll

05:23.080 --> 05:24.531
take it back from him after that.

05:27.503 --> 05:31.075
All right, thanks, Chief. Mayor, it's good to see you. Council, thank you. As Chief

05:31.575 --> 05:34.680
said, for just a few minutes today to Just go over a little bit

05:35.180 --> 05:39.224
of history and answer any questions that you may have. So I'm

05:39.724 --> 05:42.259
going to take the first three or four slides,

05:42.628 --> 05:46.032
and Chief will go from there.

05:46.113 --> 05:49.645
So Eagle Mountain joined the service area,

05:50.175 --> 05:53.322
UFSA and UFA in 2012,

05:53.964 --> 05:56.967
and so the first year that UFSA,

05:57.481 --> 06:02.075
which is the taxing side of the agreement

06:02.140 --> 06:05.852
of the 2 organizations that Eagle Mountain is a part of was

06:06.352 --> 06:10.408
in 2013. And so you can see in

06:10.908 --> 06:14.694
2013, UFSA collected $1.6

06:14.743 --> 06:18.471
million in revenue. And the revenue

06:18.971 --> 06:22.268
is mostly made up of property tax. There is

06:22.381 --> 06:26.212
a little bit of other revenue, some motor vehicle fee-in-lieu and some impact

06:26.712 --> 06:30.424
fees. that go into this number, but it's when we—

06:30.924 --> 06:35.304
when you see these numbers, it's generally the property tax. And so it was $1.6

06:35.804 --> 06:39.238
million in 2013, and you can see where it

06:39.738 --> 06:43.653
is now in 2026, up to $10.9 million.

06:44.792 --> 06:48.340
So as you're all aware, right, you are down here, lots of growth

06:48.790 --> 06:51.262
in property tax in that time.

06:52.482 --> 06:56.026
The next slide is the Expenditures, and I went the wrong way.

06:56.667 --> 06:59.905
And you can see it's of course a very similar pattern there.

07:00.594 --> 07:03.735
And so the the expenditures are mostly

07:04.235 --> 07:07.774
made up of the UFA member fee payment, which covers

07:07.870 --> 07:11.765
the staffing at the station as well as the apparatus.

07:12.070 --> 07:16.125
So the engines, trucks, and ambulances that are at

07:16.625 --> 07:20.548
your three stations. There are some miscellaneous other expenses

07:21.048 --> 07:24.045
as well. There's bond payments. For fire station construction.

07:24.719 --> 07:28.139
There's capital maintenance on our current— on the current stations,

07:28.493 --> 07:32.202
and then some other admin costs. So you can see

07:32.266 --> 07:36.408
there again, similar pattern, started with $2.1 million

07:36.745 --> 07:39.700
in total cost in 2013,

07:41.081 --> 07:45.994
and it's up to a little bit over $12 million now in 2026.

07:46.973 --> 07:49.775
So the next slide we have of puts those 2 things together.

07:50.824 --> 07:55.134
And so you can see the green line is

07:55.425 --> 07:58.985
the revenue or the total available revenue,

07:59.294 --> 08:02.848
and then the yellow line— or the yellow— the red line is the expenses.

08:04.179 --> 08:07.858
So early on, the expenditures were higher than the revenue.

08:09.384 --> 08:12.244
That changed in around 2020.

08:12.999 --> 08:15.018
And as those lines come together,

08:16.012 --> 08:20.121
UFA would add capacity,

08:20.299 --> 08:22.865
would add service to what's being provided down here.

08:23.547 --> 08:26.990
So in 2020, the 4th firefighter was

08:27.247 --> 08:31.042
added at the stations. They were staffed with 3 before that,

08:31.685 --> 08:33.583
and then an ambulance went full-time.

08:34.889 --> 08:38.048
And so you can see that that sort of brought

08:38.548 --> 08:41.079
the expenditures and the revenue back together again.

08:41.652 --> 08:44.986
2023, it sort of flipped again,

08:45.579 --> 08:49.314
and that was when UFSA raised taxes

08:50.629 --> 08:54.925
with the intention of building the 2 new fire stations and

08:54.973 --> 08:59.140
staffing the 3rd station down here in Eagle Mountain. And so for—

09:00.102 --> 09:03.340
so that took a couple years for the full cost to be realized,

09:03.853 --> 09:08.149
and you can see that in the graph in 2024 and '25.

09:08.569 --> 09:11.969
Where the revenue was a little bit ahead of the costs.

09:12.714 --> 09:15.784
And then in 2026, when the full cost of,

09:16.156 --> 09:19.197
again, the 2 stations, the bond payments,

09:19.278 --> 09:22.979
as well as the full cost of staffing that 3rd station—

09:24.204 --> 09:27.963
and you can see what that looks like now in 2026 with about

09:28.011 --> 09:31.608
$12 million in expenditures and a little under $11 million in revenue.

09:33.252 --> 09:36.765
And And this was really— this visual you have

09:37.265 --> 09:40.751
in front of you, in my view, is really kind of the biggest benefit that

09:41.089 --> 09:45.058
Eagle Mountain had as you were a part of UFSA.

09:45.284 --> 09:49.099
It allows you to leverage the rest of UFSA

09:49.326 --> 09:52.984
and get the needed service level increases before the revenue was

09:53.484 --> 09:56.904
actually being collected. And so then the last slide

09:56.953 --> 10:00.289
from me. There's a lot going on on

10:00.789 --> 10:03.123
this slide, so I'll just kind of talk you through it and we'll leave it

10:03.623 --> 10:08.061
in front of you and you can look at it. ask any questions. But the

10:08.561 --> 10:12.043
top line, the orange line, is showing

10:12.717 --> 10:17.165
the yearly or the annual shortfall or

10:17.775 --> 10:21.612
excess from the slide that we just looked at.

10:21.950 --> 10:25.611
If you recall, the first handful of years,

10:25.787 --> 10:29.432
you can see those numbers are in red, meaning there was a

10:29.801 --> 10:33.221
shortfall. Then it flips over in

10:33.333 --> 10:36.641
the early 2000s, kind of levels off.

10:36.785 --> 10:39.980
The tax increase comes, and then the additional

10:40.092 --> 10:44.283
staff station comes on, and you can see where

10:44.783 --> 10:48.120
it's sitting now. Then the black line below it

10:48.601 --> 10:51.989
just is adding those together, so the cumulative effect.

10:53.000 --> 10:56.516
You can see that in 2019, it dropped to

10:56.548 --> 10:59.570
as much as a Had $2.6 million shortfall,

11:01.159 --> 11:04.611
came back to about neutral, and then currently it's sitting

11:04.627 --> 11:05.745
about $1.1 million.

11:07.996 --> 11:12.327
So like I said, we'll leave that there and

11:12.440 --> 11:16.215
you can continue to look at that. That's the

11:16.715 --> 11:20.335
last thing for me. I'll be right here to answer any questions, but we'll

11:20.835 --> 11:22.817
turn it over to Chief to talk about a couple other things. Thank you.

11:25.697 --> 11:28.514
Okay, thanks, Tony. The, the next thing that we're going to talk about here is,

11:28.756 --> 11:32.705
is the staffing levels and how those have changed since 2013.

11:33.693 --> 11:37.678
Back then in 2013, there were 3 full-time firefighters and 3 part-time

11:37.742 --> 11:41.497
firefighters that served the entire city out of 2 stations.

11:43.089 --> 11:46.804
As you can see over the years, as Tony mentioned, the staffing level has increased

11:47.307 --> 11:49.911
when service demand increases and when revenue is available.

11:50.603 --> 11:53.679
But as of today, we're staffing the city out of 3 fire

11:54.179 --> 11:57.205
stations with 14 full-time FTEs.

11:57.302 --> 12:00.203
So, or excuse me, full-time positions, firefighters a day.

12:01.541 --> 12:05.072
And that was with the recent opening of Station 253, which is—

12:05.410 --> 12:06.877
was our most recent station here.

12:09.457 --> 12:12.913
As far as the stations go, when Eagle Mountain joined

12:13.434 --> 12:17.381
back in 2013, UFSA actually purchased Station

12:17.881 --> 12:21.212
251, which used to be right here, and Station

12:21.228 --> 12:24.915
252. And since that time, a significant number

12:25.415 --> 12:28.827
of remodels have, have occurred, and totaling in

12:28.859 --> 12:32.481
both of those expenses is about $1.3 million or so.

12:33.908 --> 12:37.323
And additionally, with that bond in 2021 that Tony mentioned,

12:37.691 --> 12:41.010
Station 251 was moved slightly down the road to serve

12:42.016 --> 12:45.338
better, serve the community better. And then also 253 was open.

12:45.403 --> 12:49.565
So significant investments in both stations

12:49.677 --> 12:50.532
and the new station.

12:53.110 --> 12:57.071
Then when we look at what apparatus were here in 2013

12:57.571 --> 13:01.071
versus what are currently here, I pulled a report

13:01.120 --> 13:03.597
from, from Tony's team.

13:04.358 --> 13:08.584
And what I could find was that back then there was a 1978

13:09.333 --> 13:12.431
pumper, multiple vehicles in that '80,

13:12.880 --> 13:16.314
'90 range, but the newest vehicle was a 2009 pickup.

13:17.197 --> 13:20.696
And there's a couple of pictures that we were able to find. These are probably

13:20.712 --> 13:23.874
from Captain Conger and all of his years here.

13:23.922 --> 13:28.030
But today we have one brand

13:28.530 --> 13:32.765
new ladder truck in the city. We have a relatively

13:33.265 --> 13:36.780
new Type 13 pumper and then a relatively new Type 1 pumper,

13:37.182 --> 13:39.561
as well as 3 ambulances in the city.

13:40.172 --> 13:43.988
So significant changes with, with both station

13:44.734 --> 13:48.238
and apparatus and how we're able to provide that service.

13:50.196 --> 13:53.503
That's, that's really the information that we wanted to share, just to give you

13:54.003 --> 13:57.747
that high-level overview as you continue to make these decisions working

13:57.843 --> 14:01.197
towards how to provide that fire and EMS service and

14:01.277 --> 14:04.344
how to fund it. And what I can say and what I want to

14:04.424 --> 14:07.748
end with is that as the fire chief at UFA, I love being down

14:07.796 --> 14:10.992
here. I love the opportunity to serve the community, and I look

14:11.492 --> 14:15.150
forward to hopefully many more years of that. So we're happy to

14:15.166 --> 14:18.458
answer any questions. The district administrator is here as well,

14:18.538 --> 14:22.311
Rachel Anderson. If there's any questions specific to her, we can open it up for

14:27.206 --> 14:29.395
Can we go back to the revenue and expenses?

14:31.994 --> 14:36.172
Sorry, this wasn't in our prep, so I haven't— I'm just

14:36.863 --> 14:39.676
taking these slides in. One more. Yeah, one more.

14:40.704 --> 14:43.714
So the costs that are being presented here,

14:43.859 --> 14:47.046
are these actual costs for Eagle Mountain?

14:47.303 --> 14:50.941
As I understand it, UFSA is a group of cities together,

14:52.236 --> 14:55.721
usually those costs kind of get apportioned out. I'm just

14:55.866 --> 14:59.592
curious if these are actual expenses incurred by Eagle Mountain or

14:59.624 --> 15:03.142
if these are prorated expenses from a larger pool.

15:03.736 --> 15:07.254
That total— what that total includes is the, is the member

15:07.754 --> 15:11.398
fee to UFA as well as the station costs and the bond payments

15:11.767 --> 15:15.558
to, to the bond carrier. So that

15:15.606 --> 15:17.967
would be if you were a direct member with UFA,

15:19.300 --> 15:22.705
That would be the amount minus the bond payments, if that's

15:22.753 --> 15:25.114
the question you're answering. And I don't know if, Tony, you have anything else to

15:25.614 --> 15:29.579
say on that. I mean, you're right on your question. I mean, this is for

15:30.079 --> 15:33.257
the most part direct costs in Eagle Mountain, but as you said,

15:33.353 --> 15:37.063
we're one organization. We make, you know, we get our

15:37.563 --> 15:39.986
property taxes all come in and we make our member fees, right? And it all

15:40.179 --> 15:44.017
comes in together, right? So there's definitely some— Other cities.

15:44.580 --> 15:48.580
Well, yeah, it's everybody's stuff in there. And so there's definitely some

15:49.335 --> 15:52.596
allocations that we have to make, right? We look at taxable

15:52.644 --> 15:56.436
value and Eagle Mountain's about 14% of the taxable value of UFSA.

15:56.516 --> 16:00.034
So for some of the expenditures that are sort

16:00.534 --> 16:03.119
of agency-wide, we have to use an allocation like that.

16:03.649 --> 16:06.653
But for like the member fee, that's pretty easy for us.

16:06.846 --> 16:10.637
You're not sharing your stations with any other members of UFA

16:10.717 --> 16:14.702
or UFSA. So Some of our cities are neighbors, so the stations

16:14.734 --> 16:18.414
are shared, you know, 50/50, 60/40. For you guys,

16:18.430 --> 16:22.351
it's 100% because you're not sharing with anybody, right? So for us,

16:22.431 --> 16:25.581
when we calculate the member fee, which is the largest piece in that,

16:26.304 --> 16:30.434
we can be certain that we know 100% of the costs are

16:30.707 --> 16:33.937
Eagle Mountain's. And so when we talk about this,

16:34.017 --> 16:37.231
the allocations part, right, we're talking about some of the

16:37.731 --> 16:40.940
admin costs, right? The administrator costs, some of those things.

16:41.004 --> 16:44.520
But the bulk of the costs we can tie directly back to Eagle

16:45.020 --> 16:48.519
Mountain. Does that help answer your question? It does, yeah. Thank you. How much is

16:48.551 --> 16:52.051
the debt service payment every year for the station

16:52.147 --> 16:55.262
houses, and how long does that bond go on?

16:55.776 --> 16:59.068
Yeah, so we have to— Are you specific on Eagle Mountain Station, or are you

16:59.568 --> 17:03.370
talking— Yeah, our debt service. Ours, because there's the bond covers Covered

17:03.867 --> 17:07.298
our stations plus 2 others. How much

17:07.346 --> 17:10.087
is our portion of the debt service payment?

17:11.017 --> 17:14.384
So again, we have to do a little of the allocation there. But if you

17:14.884 --> 17:19.321
look at the 2021 bond, it was, it was about $37

17:19.353 --> 17:23.313
million total and the annual payments in

17:23.361 --> 17:26.663
the $2.3 to $2.4 depending on the year.

17:27.192 --> 17:30.753
And the 2 stations, took about a little over half

17:31.253 --> 17:34.477
of the constructions. And so the bond payment on

17:34.509 --> 17:38.570
the 2021 bond for Eagle Mountain is about $1.2 million.

17:39.549 --> 17:42.775
Um, and then the— for the 2016 bond, you have no

17:43.032 --> 17:46.675
direct stations on that one, and so we have

17:46.691 --> 17:50.254
to allocate that piece using that taxable value.

17:50.463 --> 17:54.572
And so that 14%, and so you're about $350

17:55.000 --> 17:58.335
The 2016 bond

17:58.835 --> 18:02.606
expires in 2035, and the 2021

18:03.106 --> 18:06.321
bond expires in 2041. They're both

18:06.337 --> 18:10.180
20-year debts. With that in mind,

18:10.196 --> 18:13.658
Tony, the 14%, is that this year's

18:14.158 --> 18:17.280
taxable value? Seems like we're at 11 or 12, just going off memory. I don't

18:17.780 --> 18:20.415
remember. 14 point— well, that's the 5-year moving average.

18:21.567 --> 18:26.071
average. In 2026, you're about, yeah, about 14.5%

18:26.265 --> 18:29.833
of the total taxable value of UFSA is Eagle Mountain.

18:30.350 --> 18:36.178
That's right.

18:37.481 --> 18:41.290
I mean, I'm going to

18:41.467 --> 18:45.276
ask some obvious questions because I feel like maybe I'm missing some pieces

18:45.776 --> 18:49.651
of this. So we're talking about UFSA perhaps leaving

18:49.684 --> 18:52.912
that organization and doing a contract. What does that

18:53.412 --> 18:57.194
look like for us as far as our financial obligation when we have the

18:57.694 --> 19:01.561
bonds that you're talking about? You've talked about the member fees, which would obviously

19:01.641 --> 19:04.874
be paying for personnel, equipment, et cetera.

19:06.904 --> 19:11.237
So the obligations to UFSA, if you— if

19:11.302 --> 19:15.199
and when you choose to separate, will be the bond payment for

19:15.232 --> 19:19.342
the 2 stations that were— that are— so those— that $1.2

19:19.842 --> 19:23.549
plus the $350,000, right? So the 2 outstanding bonds Eagle

19:24.049 --> 19:26.648
Mountain would need to continue. So we'll complete the bond basically. You need to continue

19:27.148 --> 19:30.871
your portion of the bond. And then 252 is the other station

19:31.160 --> 19:35.607
UFSA owns that was purchased from Eagle Mountain in 2013.

19:36.346 --> 19:39.911
So there would need to be a negotiation on how Eagle

19:40.411 --> 19:44.294
Mountain purchases that back from UFSA. Let me hang on

19:44.794 --> 19:48.244
one second. So is the, the actual land for each of

19:48.744 --> 19:52.290
the, um, the fire stations something that

19:52.756 --> 19:56.545
normally when a city contracts with you that they buy that portion

19:56.577 --> 20:00.062
of it and then they're just doing a contract for services and equipment?

20:00.286 --> 20:05.554
Yeah, all the direct members own their own stations, and since UFSA's Acting

20:05.570 --> 20:08.848
as the direct member for Eagle Mountain and Mill Creek and all of those,

20:09.442 --> 20:13.524
UFSA owns all the stations, but Harriman

20:14.024 --> 20:17.171
owns their stations, Riverton owns theirs, Cottonwood Heights, Holiday, etc.,

20:18.184 --> 20:21.928
owns their stations. And so as a direct member, you—that's why—that's why

20:21.960 --> 20:25.527
you need to continue with the bond payment because you take

20:25.575 --> 20:28.789
over the ownership, and that's why the discussion

20:29.289 --> 20:31.370
on 252 needs to happen. as part of this separation.

20:33.060 --> 20:36.120
Okay, go ahead. What were you saying? I was just going to say there's a

20:36.620 --> 20:39.620
lot of that still isn't worked out, to your question, and that's why Rachel's here,

20:40.120 --> 20:41.720
and that's something that we're going to start working forward on.

20:44.260 --> 20:47.880
Is it okay if I don't, or can I keep going? Go ahead. Sorry,

20:48.380 --> 20:50.300
I didn't mean to cut you off. No, no, I wasn't cut off. I just—

20:50.800 --> 20:54.520
it's good to have the discussion. What are— who are the top 3 cities

20:55.020 --> 20:58.537
with UFSA? Top 3 in which way? In terms

20:59.037 --> 21:01.842
of what? Population size. Mill Creek,

21:02.724 --> 21:06.879
Taylorsville, and Eagle Mountain would

21:07.379 --> 21:10.810
be the top 3. And then we have Midvale and Kearns

21:11.310 --> 21:14.115
and Magna that would kind of be the next. Are those same 3 the same

21:14.179 --> 21:17.789
thing for expenditures as well? Yeah. Okay.

21:18.928 --> 21:22.217
It just depends on how many station crews that they have

21:22.717 --> 21:26.197
in there. station or in their city. Mill Creek has 3 like

21:26.697 --> 21:29.759
you. Taylorsville has 3 like you. Midville only has 2.

21:30.642 --> 21:34.606
So, um, so yeah, it just depends on how many crews

21:34.798 --> 21:38.281
run out of the city. And in, in the last fiscal cycle, I'm just

21:38.329 --> 21:42.164
curious what, what, uh, by comparison, what is the taxable

21:42.664 --> 21:45.310
value of each of those cities? I mean, compare us to the top 3.

21:45.936 --> 21:48.696
Uh, I don't, I don't have that off the top of my head. I know

21:49.196 --> 21:52.421
Mill Creek's Taxable value is by far the largest

21:52.533 --> 21:56.258
in UFSA. They were like 17%? Yeah, 18%.

21:56.370 --> 21:59.710
Or they might have— just UFSA, they might be in the, in the 20s.

22:00.176 --> 22:03.676
So you have the unincorporated Salt Lake County has a

22:04.176 --> 22:07.819
large portion of the taxable value too. They don't have the population,

22:08.413 --> 22:11.367
but they have a large taxable value. They might be second.

22:12.363 --> 22:15.791
And then Eagle Mountain is probably pretty similar to like Taylorsville.

22:17.800 --> 22:21.062
But don't 100% quote me on that. I'm going a little bit off the

22:21.562 --> 22:23.985
top of my head. So, but Mill Creek's for sure the largest.

22:28.474 --> 22:31.891
I'm just thinking about the member cities and just how many

22:32.391 --> 22:36.455
of those that you serve are members of the USFA

22:36.535 --> 22:39.835
versus just those who contract with you?

22:41.332 --> 22:45.005
Members that are direct members. The remaining 12 or 13

22:45.037 --> 22:48.696
members are members through UFSA. Who are

22:48.728 --> 22:51.448
the 4 cities that are direct? Cottonwood Heights,

22:51.885 --> 22:54.129
Holiday, Riverton, and Herriman.

22:58.574 --> 23:02.136
Thank you. And Herriman

23:02.636 --> 23:05.953
and Riverton became direct members in 2020. They were,

23:06.325 --> 23:09.577
were a part of UFSA Cottonwood Heights and Holiday

23:10.077 --> 23:11.089
have always been direct.

23:14.385 --> 23:17.217
Which year did you say Riverton and Herriman became direct members?

23:26.663 --> 23:29.861
2020. Sorry, one other question. When, when tax increases happen,

23:30.378 --> 23:33.727
can you walk us through that process? Yeah,

23:33.743 --> 23:36.164
I mean, I think you guys just went through one. It would be exactly the

23:36.664 --> 23:40.524
same. Yeah. So we are a calendar year entity, so we have

23:40.556 --> 23:44.628
to, you know, make our intention known in October time.

23:45.670 --> 23:47.641
We go through the budget process.

23:48.988 --> 23:52.691
The budget gets adopted in December, and we hold a tax

23:52.819 --> 23:57.035
hearing in December as well. We have to do all of the advertising

23:58.350 --> 24:01.765
in newspapers. We have to send out postcards to all the residents, to all the

24:02.265 --> 24:05.961
parcels, things like that. Is that what you're after?

24:06.428 --> 24:10.376
I mean, really, and then the process, right? It starts with the board looking at

24:10.876 --> 24:14.481
the budget and deciding that, okay, we need a little more revenue. The last time

24:14.981 --> 24:18.742
we did it, it was because we were expanding service, right? We needed to

24:19.016 --> 24:22.535
pay for the new bond that we just built the 4 stations

24:22.567 --> 24:25.266
with. We needed to staff 253 out here.

24:26.368 --> 24:30.158
Had a station in Kearns that we weren't staffing. We started

24:30.658 --> 24:34.253
staffing that. So there was like a 3 or 4 year plan to build

24:34.317 --> 24:38.155
up to, okay, all right, we're ready now. Station 253 is

24:38.251 --> 24:41.865
built. Now's the time to go through the tax increase so we

24:42.365 --> 24:45.783
can get these employees hired, trained, and start staffing the station. So each

24:45.895 --> 24:48.931
city is— you set a different rate,

24:49.268 --> 24:52.881
I would imagine, or you set the same rate? No, UFSA has one rate.

24:53.106 --> 24:57.124
One rate. Everybody. So it's 0.001756,

24:57.221 --> 25:00.883
I think. For everybody. Eagle Mountain residents pay that, Mill Creek.

25:01.270 --> 25:03.899
When we raise taxes, it's the same across the board.

25:04.076 --> 25:07.315
Everybody has one rate. And it's even the same down here in Utah

25:07.815 --> 25:11.133
County versus Salt Lake. They equalize that rate across the whole

25:11.150 --> 25:15.168
service area. Okay. And in the 13 years that

25:15.540 --> 25:19.040
going back in time, it looks like there's been a rate increase

25:19.540 --> 25:22.880
pretty Consistently? I don't think there was one this year. Just 3 total in that

25:23.380 --> 25:26.749
whole time. 3, oh, OK. So once in 2018. So 3 increases.

25:26.862 --> 25:30.186
Once in 2018, and then the, the 2 we did back

25:30.686 --> 25:33.911
to back for all the reasons that I just talked about in '24 and '25.

25:34.007 --> 25:38.021
Other than that, there's been no Truth in Tax. OK, and in those 3 rate

25:38.521 --> 25:42.180
increases, did you ever have a referendum? No. In any of those

25:42.212 --> 25:43.111
3? OK.

25:46.370 --> 25:49.866
Well, that— yeah, that's kind of my thought process. It's, it's,

25:49.963 --> 25:53.862
it's difficult. It would be difficult for citizens to have a referendum

25:54.542 --> 25:57.704
on a large— an entity that large. Okay, thank you.

26:04.588 --> 26:08.081
You had a slide— sorry, did I cut somebody out? Um, you had

26:08.581 --> 26:12.058
a slide that had the different apparatus on it and just kind of the state

26:12.139 --> 26:13.906
of what we had previously.

26:15.530 --> 26:18.424
How often— I know apparatus are very expensive,

26:18.536 --> 26:21.913
and it takes a while for them to even just get, like, built after

26:22.413 --> 26:25.898
you order them and stuff like that. How frequently of a

26:26.398 --> 26:30.658
cadence are your different communities

26:30.771 --> 26:33.857
getting new apparatus or changing things out?

26:34.442 --> 26:36.765
I'll take a shot at that, or you can just have it.

26:38.807 --> 26:42.580
We have a capital replacement plan that replaces— the heavy apparatus

26:43.080 --> 26:46.963
are typically on a 9-year lifespan. Okay. Those get rotated through 'cause

26:46.995 --> 26:50.543
we have busy stations that run the wheels off and slow stations that

26:51.043 --> 26:55.102
don't. So we have a kind of a replacement pattern. Even when they go out

26:55.602 --> 26:58.715
of, you know, let's say a Mill Creek area, they could go to Copperton and

26:59.215 --> 27:03.082
get a few more years out of 'em. But we basically buy

27:03.130 --> 27:06.983
about 5 Type 1 engines, 3 ladder trucks,

27:07.064 --> 27:10.451
and 5 ambulances every 3 years. So that's the number that

27:10.951 --> 27:14.594
we need to circulate through. So everybody, all of our stations now are equipped

27:15.094 --> 27:18.496
with, with, you know, within the last 5 years have had something new in their

27:18.528 --> 27:22.125
station. How do you ensure that across all the different communities

27:22.141 --> 27:25.657
that you serve that it's balanced and no one

27:25.769 --> 27:28.900
community ends up with all the new and no one community ends up with all

27:29.400 --> 27:32.658
the old? That's— they've got like— that's part of that replacement cycle. Yeah.

27:33.236 --> 27:36.832
But they're— they are all, again, with our plan that's been in place

27:37.332 --> 27:41.168
for about 9, 10 years now, the capital replacement plan, we've been able to cycle

27:41.668 --> 27:45.599
through everything. Many, many years ago, we bought all brand new ambulances and all brand

27:46.099 --> 27:49.373
new heavy apparatus. And so that replacement was coming up and the

27:49.873 --> 27:53.146
total would have been $30 to $40 million to replace them all at once again.

27:53.612 --> 27:57.668
So we implemented that. Rotational plan to every 5 years by a handful.

27:58.215 --> 28:01.033
I don't know who's going to answer this question, but I'll ask it and I'll

28:01.533 --> 28:04.770
let you guys work it out. You mentioned that everyone has the same tax rate

28:05.270 --> 28:08.442
across all of the UFSA cities.

28:10.319 --> 28:14.102
How is it determined which of those cities got to have a 3-man

28:14.198 --> 28:17.978
team for years and why some got to have 4-man team?

28:18.783 --> 28:22.123
The, the decision is, is on the member. So being

28:22.460 --> 28:26.171
UFSA is one member, the direction from the board is to push for

28:26.671 --> 28:30.186
four-handed staffing in all of the stations. There's only 2 UFSA

28:30.686 --> 28:34.025
stations that don't have it right now. One of them's in Immigration Canyon

28:34.525 --> 28:38.506
and one's in Copperton. Okay. And the call volume there is not high

28:38.522 --> 28:42.008
enough necessarily to warrant that. It's still a, it's still

28:42.508 --> 28:45.782
a goal. But all of the other direct members, Holiday, Conewood Heights,

28:45.927 --> 28:49.617
Riverton, and Herriman, get to decide that, and that's how they can control their

28:50.117 --> 28:53.958
costs. Do any of them decide to do 3-man teams instead

28:53.990 --> 28:58.326
of 4, or are they all on 4? Only Cottonwood Heights has one 3-person

28:58.826 --> 29:00.490
staffed engine. All right, thank you.

29:03.696 --> 29:06.351
Anything else? Okay,

29:06.576 --> 29:09.649
thank you for your time. Appreciate it. Yeah, thank you.

29:10.834 --> 29:14.036
Can I just say one last— yeah, I just want to say thank you for

29:14.536 --> 29:17.646
the service that you have provided us. We're grateful for it, and I think it's

29:17.662 --> 29:20.935
been— I think it's been top-notch. I mean, I went to your fire school,

29:21.031 --> 29:23.454
I enjoyed that. I just want you guys to know that I'm grateful for the

29:23.954 --> 29:27.898
service that your firefighters have provided to Eagle Mountain City. Thank you. I was going

29:28.010 --> 29:31.186
to exactly say the same thing. I mean, there's, there's no doubt every single

29:31.459 --> 29:34.909
council meeting our council members are extending appreciation to their

29:35.409 --> 29:39.064
first responders. You guys do an exceptionally good job for us. We're very much appreciative

29:39.080 --> 29:42.562
for all the work that I was also gonna just ask

29:42.610 --> 29:45.886
if you could send those slides to us. That would be greatly appreciated. Yep,

29:45.902 --> 29:49.527
we'll shoot them your way. Okay,

29:49.592 --> 29:52.685
and I was waiting till the end to say it's been a pleasure to be

29:52.814 --> 29:56.166
on the board. It's fun, and at no point ever

29:56.198 --> 29:59.728
have we as a body or me on the board

30:00.163 --> 30:03.882
not been really appreciative to be a member of UFA and UFSA.

30:04.175 --> 30:08.700
I think it's been a privilege and an honor, We love the service and appreciate

30:09.200 --> 30:12.779
everything we get, and especially from the boots on the ground too.

30:12.875 --> 30:16.231
I mean, these guys already know, but I'm eating dinner

30:16.731 --> 30:19.234
Saturday with the 251, no, no, no,

30:19.539 --> 30:23.087
252. Yeah. I was with 253 the other night.

30:23.135 --> 30:24.998
I mean, I hang out with these guys all the time when I get a

30:25.498 --> 30:28.819
chance, and it's just an honor to be out there with these guys,

30:29.959 --> 30:33.966
and thanks for your leadership too. And Tony for all the numbers and everybody

30:33.982 --> 30:37.593
that supports us. Even the— I appreciate you making the drive down

30:37.609 --> 30:40.840
here, by the way. Thank you very much.

30:41.115 --> 30:44.636
Yeah, thanks. All right.

30:45.318 --> 30:46.989
No other discussion or questions on that, right?

30:49.730 --> 30:53.558
I was going to, just for everybody's sake, state some other facts,

30:53.574 --> 30:56.802
but it doesn't— I don't think it matters and we covered it, so we'll move

30:57.302 --> 31:00.637
on. I want to go back to 1A. I mentioned that we're going to push

31:00.750 --> 31:03.977
that back to 10/20. I just want to

31:04.477 --> 31:07.816
know if there's any notes specifically that we can say,

31:07.832 --> 31:11.438
hey, maybe look at this or look at that, so when it comes

31:11.470 --> 31:14.948
back, it's maybe a little bit more prepared

31:15.448 --> 31:19.669
or ready. Is there anything specific that you want to

31:19.766 --> 31:22.989
ask if there's some— Go ahead.

31:23.100 --> 31:26.164
Yeah, I was just gonna say, I think that what I'm

31:26.664 --> 31:30.977
trying to balance in my mind is there are, in my neighborhood,

31:30.993 --> 31:34.892
for example, there's a property that has 6 trailers

31:35.309 --> 31:38.614
lined up. I can't imagine anyone owning 6 trailers.

31:38.871 --> 31:42.224
So I have to imagine they either have a lot of family members or

31:42.724 --> 31:46.219
it's become kind of a community parking lot. It's hard

31:46.719 --> 31:49.555
to tell who's renting what. There was a, uh,

31:50.261 --> 31:54.477
a, uh, someone who posted, I think they have some acreage, and they just posted

31:54.977 --> 31:58.084
on the Eagle Mountain Citizens page, hey, you know, rent out

31:58.584 --> 32:02.348
my land, come just park your trailer here. And so

32:02.848 --> 32:06.115
I think there needs to be some definition around what the code— to give our

32:06.615 --> 32:10.331
code enforcement folks some actual tangibles

32:10.444 --> 32:13.874
of, I don't know if we're looking up license plate numbers to see if they're

32:14.374 --> 32:18.033
registered. To that property owner. But I also understand

32:18.065 --> 32:22.064
there's situations where maybe you do have 5 acres and a family member is

32:22.145 --> 32:24.972
parking their RV. So they're, you know,

32:25.052 --> 32:28.714
potentially for larger lots might be some room to have

32:28.746 --> 32:32.007
more than maybe 1 or 2 recreational vehicles. But as,

32:32.826 --> 32:37.002
you know, smaller lots, it just doesn't look great and

32:37.083 --> 32:40.920
it does impact property values people

32:40.936 --> 32:43.277
that are their next-door neighbor maybe trying to sell their house.

32:44.319 --> 32:48.263
So that's the balance I think I'm trying to strike in my mind is what

32:48.763 --> 32:52.255
code should exist to do that. So is there something that was in the packet

32:52.755 --> 32:54.723
that you think needs to kind of be worked on a little bit so we

32:55.223 --> 32:57.801
can have some specific direction to tie it to? How to enforce?

32:58.587 --> 33:01.793
How to enforce as well. That was something that I kind

33:01.857 --> 33:05.577
of was wondering based off of what you said. It's like how

33:05.705 --> 33:09.221
would How would staff even begin to know if it's a relative or not is

33:09.477 --> 33:13.572
one of the questions that I have. So Brandon, did the code

33:14.072 --> 33:17.120
enforcement guys, have they weighed into this? I just want

33:17.620 --> 33:20.573
to know if they feel like, or if they're going to be here to answer

33:21.073 --> 33:23.768
if they thought that this is going to be, you know, how they would go

33:24.268 --> 33:27.573
about enforcing it. Yeah, they want some more direction.

33:27.637 --> 33:31.282
The current code, they feel kind of is

33:31.782 --> 33:34.907
vague. Vague enough that it doesn't help them as much as it could.

33:35.084 --> 33:38.788
So I think that's what— between now and the 20th,

33:39.670 --> 33:42.557
are they going to have a chance to say this isn't going to work so

33:43.057 --> 33:46.390
that we can get a little bit further down the road? I know that the

33:46.890 --> 33:50.624
attached code that was in your packet, they had

33:51.602 --> 33:55.307
neighborhood improvement work with David Stroud to prepare that, and so they

33:55.807 --> 33:59.071
felt like that— They like it. that code. So they have worked

33:59.571 --> 34:03.350
on this issue, and they, they— that, yeah, that, that code was based

34:03.850 --> 34:04.464
on working with them.

34:08.452 --> 34:11.761
Did it go? Yeah. I mean, I just want to bring up the fact that

34:12.261 --> 34:16.086
we've already reviewed this and denied almost identically what it says

34:16.119 --> 34:19.052
in here, uh, within the last 12 months. Um,

34:20.791 --> 34:23.753
I am never going to be in favor of us trying to have our code

34:24.253 --> 34:27.844
enforcement try to establish licensure? Are we going to go start knocking on

34:27.956 --> 34:31.786
doors and having them provide documents? I think that that's not even feasible.

34:32.587 --> 34:35.647
So I think if we're going to spend time on this, I think we ought

34:36.147 --> 34:38.740
to make sure that there's support from at least a majority of people. Otherwise,

34:38.788 --> 34:42.265
I think it's a waste of time. So, so are you— so are you suggesting

34:42.297 --> 34:45.390
then that you'd be okay with someone parking

34:45.406 --> 34:48.114
as many trailers as they can on their lot? That's not what I said.

34:48.450 --> 34:51.190
Well, that's why I'm asking the clarifying question. I don't think there's a way for

34:51.690 --> 34:54.064
you to be able to determine that? How are you going to determine if they're

34:54.564 --> 34:57.950
doing it for a friend? How many is too many? Are you going to base

34:58.450 --> 35:00.808
it on size of lot? Are you going to base it on what your personal

35:00.824 --> 35:04.501
perspective is on a piece of land? I think that there's

35:05.001 --> 35:08.660
too many variables in there that become worldview rather than

35:09.077 --> 35:12.626
it's actually creating a nuisance. You know,

35:12.770 --> 35:16.062
I talked with Brett about this before. I mean, we've had this discussion at

35:16.562 --> 35:20.090
length. I am not in favor of

35:20.122 --> 35:23.283
being overly punitive for

35:23.783 --> 35:27.342
how people use their property. When people buy large properties,

35:27.616 --> 35:31.611
interesting enough, Brett drove around and identified many

35:31.885 --> 35:35.490
trailers on pieces of property that would probably

35:35.523 --> 35:37.461
be shocking to the people sitting on the dais here.

35:38.822 --> 35:42.532
And so I think perhaps we

35:42.548 --> 35:45.352
ought to make sure that our own houses are in order before we do something

35:48.276 --> 35:52.563
So, so you— if there were a way to enforce

35:53.655 --> 35:57.124
well enough, you'd be open to some kind of— I just think we become an

35:57.204 --> 36:00.479
HOA at that point, and you know how I feel about HOAs. Let them use

36:00.979 --> 36:03.932
their property unless they're doing something that's a nuisance. Yeah,

36:03.948 --> 36:07.560
having 12 trailers on there is probably not appropriate, but what is the right

36:08.060 --> 36:11.237
number and who gets to make that determination? Well,

36:11.253 --> 36:13.485
and then I, and I think that's the purpose of bringing it back for a

36:13.985 --> 36:16.969
discussion, to see, to see if we could figure out a nuanced way to,

36:17.194 --> 36:20.518
to handle that. Is there 3 people who want to do that? Because the staff's

36:20.630 --> 36:24.147
already been through this cycle, and how this is written is

36:24.179 --> 36:27.583
no different than what we said no to. I'm, I'm open for

36:27.615 --> 36:31.131
discussion. I am, I am as well. I, I am

36:31.244 --> 36:35.338
too, because I think that we have zoning for a reason. I feel like

36:35.838 --> 36:38.069
there's people who are on our council that have said that to me in the

36:38.569 --> 36:42.376
past. And whether it's commercial or residential really

36:42.568 --> 36:47.196
dictates the use that you can use for it. And if you're renting out 37

36:47.791 --> 36:51.165
slots for 37 RVs on

36:51.665 --> 36:54.909
your 1 or 2-acre piece of property, that's probably extending

36:55.006 --> 36:58.750
past what residential is. Um, I don't want to be punitive.

36:58.862 --> 37:01.690
I want people to be able to use their land, but I also think we

37:02.190 --> 37:06.034
have a due duty to make sure that their neighbor's property rights

37:06.534 --> 37:09.928
are protected as well. So I would like to discuss it. And I've,

37:10.088 --> 37:13.389
I've been thinking about this too. I mean, and I did have some conversations

37:13.889 --> 37:16.803
with Council Member Wood about this. And for me, I'm just— I,

37:16.995 --> 37:20.841
I'm not sure that this is the right approach. From my

37:21.341 --> 37:25.088
perspective, I'd like to see us perhaps revisit our home-based

37:25.588 --> 37:28.407
business code and see if there's not something that we could already do to that

37:28.519 --> 37:32.357
code to tighten it up a little bit, create some definitions.

37:32.614 --> 37:36.468
Because for me, the substantive difference is this: is it

37:36.968 --> 37:40.644
a residential use in a residential neighborhood by a resident that

37:41.144 --> 37:44.916
owns the property, or is it a semi-commercial use or

37:45.416 --> 37:48.304
something that's grown beyond the scale of a residential use? And how do you tell

37:48.368 --> 37:51.918
the difference between the two? I'm not sure how you do that, but I think

37:52.046 --> 37:56.431
that's the— I think that's the gray area that causes the I

37:56.543 --> 37:59.418
think in my mind, if I see a trailer,

38:00.398 --> 38:03.562
which is pretty common throughout the city, you'll see it on a side yard or

38:03.868 --> 38:07.803
no big deal. But when you see 5 or 6 trailers

38:08.303 --> 38:12.654
on a property, I'm like, what is the personal

38:12.686 --> 38:16.059
use that's not screened? But Zach, I went by one of

38:16.559 --> 38:18.870
my neighbors on the way to the— on the way to the building today,

38:18.886 --> 38:23.233
and they had 6 trailers sitting there. There was a dump trailer, there's a UTV

38:23.281 --> 38:25.590
hauler, there's a camp trailer,

38:26.343 --> 38:29.694
there's a— I can't remember all of them— a horse trailer. I mean,

38:29.710 --> 38:32.820
there's just— so— But I think to blanket

38:32.836 --> 38:36.363
that is, I think there are some folks who probably have

38:36.863 --> 38:40.210
a need for that and it's their personal residence. And are we going to say

38:40.932 --> 38:43.769
2 and you're done, that's a problem? I'm not willing to go there.

38:44.539 --> 38:47.971
But I, I, but I But I also think it's a real problem

38:48.471 --> 38:51.966
when we have some of our residents who are saying, I'm going to house 30

38:51.998 --> 38:56.009
or 40 trailers that don't belong to me, charge a rent for that,

38:56.892 --> 39:00.277
and subject all of my neighbors to the traffic and to the blight that

39:00.325 --> 39:03.758
that brings. So I think there is a balance that we need to find.

39:04.111 --> 39:07.801
Agreed. And I think that, you know, the different types

39:08.301 --> 39:11.974
of trailers, you know, there's like my— I have family He's

39:12.474 --> 39:16.604
a brick mason. He's got an RV trailer, OHV, all good.

39:16.717 --> 39:19.691
I think here this is talking about— Yeah,

39:20.640 --> 39:23.917
probably a wheelbarrow on a trailer that you mix concrete in, right? But this,

39:24.207 --> 39:28.082
this specifically I think identifies a recreational

39:28.098 --> 39:29.761
vehicle, something that you can live in,

39:31.021 --> 39:35.091
vacation in. You know, a horse trailer wouldn't qualify under this definition

39:35.139 --> 39:39.690
that I'm reading. So we're specifically looking for You

39:40.190 --> 39:43.039
know, fifth wheels, motorhome, truck camper,

39:43.103 --> 39:46.115
camping trailer, travel trailer. That's what it's identified in here.

39:46.564 --> 39:50.154
And I think that kind of narrows the list of what's being rented

39:50.654 --> 39:53.583
out, at least in my mind. But I think that it's— I think the problem

39:53.695 --> 39:57.092
is bigger in a very minor way and small way. I mean, we have lots

39:57.204 --> 40:01.034
that have half a dozen dump trucks or some

40:01.146 --> 40:04.495
big commercial vehicles that are being stored or that

40:04.995 --> 40:08.314
come come every morning and leave every night. And it's, it's a

40:08.426 --> 40:12.036
commercial hub of business in a residential area. And so I just

40:12.536 --> 40:15.983
think those are the things that, that are, that are operating outside

40:16.483 --> 40:19.288
of what I believe should be happening in a residential neighborhood.

40:19.994 --> 40:23.122
And so I don't know if this is the perfect approach,

40:23.908 --> 40:27.262
but that's the problem that I think we ought to tackle rather than being

40:27.358 --> 40:30.550
so myopically focused on how many trailers and what type of trailers.

40:30.662 --> 40:34.333
It's like, how can we allow people to

40:34.833 --> 40:37.171
use their property in the way that they choose? And if they have 6 trailers

40:37.671 --> 40:42.012
and they're their trailers, who cares? I don't care. But I do care when

40:42.188 --> 40:45.603
it becomes an issue for the neighbors. It's perhaps— perhaps some screening

40:45.731 --> 40:49.193
needs to be considered. I mean, I've thought about that. Maybe some screening requirements

40:49.693 --> 40:52.976
if you're going to do something that's a little more excessive. Maybe those trips per

40:53.089 --> 40:57.161
day that we talk about in the, in the in

40:57.257 --> 41:00.790
the home-based business code. I mean, once you generate, I don't remember the number,

41:00.806 --> 41:04.226
15 or 20 trips a day, you're no longer a home-based business.

41:04.435 --> 41:07.630
If you've got more than one employee and yourself that's working there,

41:07.694 --> 41:10.921
you're outside of the code function. So I think there's some other

41:11.421 --> 41:15.449
mechanisms that we could evaluate and perhaps tweak to allow

41:16.060 --> 41:19.656
some of this to be used in a way that

41:19.737 --> 41:23.301
is meaningful for the residents, But detrimental to

41:23.430 --> 41:26.384
commercial operations that are happening in our residential neighborhoods.

41:27.861 --> 41:31.442
I just want to make one clarification here. The definition for the recreation

41:31.474 --> 41:35.119
vehicles include fifth-wheel trailers,

41:35.488 --> 41:39.374
boat, OHV, self-propelled, used for recreation. So it's

41:39.422 --> 41:43.709
not just livable trailers in 10.10.090.

41:44.576 --> 41:47.161
So I think that encompasses a lot of the things that we were talking about.

41:47.370 --> 41:51.601
I just My

41:52.101 --> 41:56.280
biggest problem with this is that this is really not about a nuisance

41:56.780 --> 42:00.282
that it's causing to adjoining people. It's more or

42:00.460 --> 42:04.144
less people, I don't like the look of it. That's such

42:04.193 --> 42:07.896
a subjective thing. And I think we have

42:08.121 --> 42:11.390
way too much of that, frankly. We start to impede on

42:11.422 --> 42:14.924
people's ability to use the property they're spending hundreds hundreds of thousands of dollars on,

42:15.662 --> 42:18.856
uh, to be able to live there without everybody else telling them how to live.

42:19.626 --> 42:23.317
So for me, look, I get it

42:23.847 --> 42:27.041
if they have a trailer on every speck of their property.

42:27.538 --> 42:31.069
I think it's pretty obvious there's an issue there. If we're going to go

42:31.569 --> 42:35.162
forward with this, then let's set some guidelines that are achievable. I think

42:35.242 --> 42:38.676
asking our code enforcement to go chase down

42:38.869 --> 42:42.391
registrations is not suitable for them. That's too much.

42:43.540 --> 42:46.001
And I think they would probably agree with that. When we went through that before,

42:46.066 --> 42:49.560
they weren't in favor of that. To Brett's point with

42:50.060 --> 42:54.719
the home businesses, again, you're still going to have to establish— look,

42:54.736 --> 42:57.485
they've got 6 trailers on there. Are we going to go knock on the door

42:57.985 --> 43:00.954
and ask them if they have a business license to operate those? They say they're

43:01.035 --> 43:03.403
all my family. How are you going to determine one way or the other?

43:03.970 --> 43:07.445
Would you, would you be open to screening Requirements. I think screening is

43:07.542 --> 43:10.833
fine. I even said to Brett, maybe there's, uh, you can look at— and

43:11.090 --> 43:14.623
again, I'm not in favor of being punitive in any way, but maybe

43:15.123 --> 43:19.006
there's a lot size that allows for a certain envelope of that property

43:19.103 --> 43:22.298
to be utilized for vehicular storage that is

43:22.474 --> 43:25.541
running, or trailers, or whatever. Maybe that's, maybe that's

43:26.041 --> 43:29.395
a direction that we can at least talk about. But yeah, they should

43:29.895 --> 43:33.634
be screened. But if they get to agricultural and

43:34.134 --> 43:36.525
rural and we're talking that big, I mean, how are you going to screen 5

43:37.025 --> 43:40.284
acres, 10 acres? I think that there needs to be a further conversation.

43:41.022 --> 43:44.251
But I think this all leads back to— I think we can workshop this and

43:44.751 --> 43:48.571
try to reach a better spot than where we currently are. And so,

43:48.667 --> 43:51.815
and then, so do we do that in another work session? Yeah,

43:52.281 --> 43:56.136
just move it to the 20th. I guess I was wondering if there's anything specifically

43:56.636 --> 44:00.760
that— and there's good Is there enough

44:01.260 --> 44:04.524
time? So like, we're going to need— like, 15 minutes won't solve the problem,

44:04.960 --> 44:07.513
and 30 minutes might not. We might need 45 minutes in a work session,

44:08.922 --> 44:11.175
maybe an hour. I don't know what else is on the docket that day.

44:13.037 --> 44:16.812
I was just going to say, it sounds like policing is probably not

44:16.828 --> 44:20.363
going to be a viable option for code enforcement anyway. So maybe we're

44:20.863 --> 44:22.419
talking about lot sizes,

44:26.090 --> 44:28.782
I would maybe go one step further. If you feel strongly about this,

44:29.346 --> 44:32.135
maybe 2 of you can go and work with David to figure that out and

44:32.635 --> 44:36.862
then bring it for the rest of us to review. Yeah, I think maybe that's

44:37.362 --> 44:40.414
what I was looking for, because what I was getting at, Councilmember Wood, is what

44:40.914 --> 44:44.495
you said, is we've looked at this and what, what could David

44:44.995 --> 44:48.506
do any different than, uh, this got put back on the priority list.

44:49.260 --> 44:53.466
And we're trying to figure out how we move it forward, and it's still— it's

44:53.966 --> 44:57.284
tough. So I know, Councilmember Heros, just something you— I think you're the one that

44:57.784 --> 45:01.080
put it on there. So would you work throughout the next couple weeks to get

45:01.714 --> 45:03.861
this in a good place?

45:07.786 --> 45:10.792
He's not jumping forward.

45:11.180 --> 45:14.605
I'm just thinking about my plate. We could Huh?

45:14.880 --> 45:18.454
I said all of a sudden everyone wants to table it. No, I'm just kidding.

45:18.535 --> 45:20.508
No, I'll work with you, Councilmember Hewish.

45:22.475 --> 45:25.824
Yeah, we'll get it done. Maybe, Melissa, it would

45:25.856 --> 45:28.448
be good for you to give some input. I mean, you voted against it the

45:28.948 --> 45:32.223
last time, but if you see something differently now, maybe you should give some input

45:32.723 --> 45:36.135
as to why. I'll share this since we won't be able to work as a

45:36.635 --> 45:38.058
quorum, but I have,

45:40.679 --> 45:45.120
had multiple businesses talk to me about how it's unfair that

45:45.152 --> 45:49.367
some people are operating commercial entities out of their home and

45:49.880 --> 45:53.327
they are kind of paying less for

45:53.827 --> 45:56.901
their land, paying less for their taxes, all of those things.

45:57.479 --> 46:00.989
And then there's ones who are following the letter of the law and doing everything

46:01.085 --> 46:04.452
the way they're supposed to. And they're the ones that are

46:04.952 --> 46:07.922
following the law are at a disadvantage to the ones that are just kind of

46:07.954 --> 46:11.830
doing things under the table and not within code. So I

46:12.330 --> 46:15.706
think that might be something to look at, because I do think Councilmember

46:16.206 --> 46:19.601
Wright has a point that this may have more to do with home-based

46:19.713 --> 46:23.058
business requirements than how many trailers per square foot.

46:28.332 --> 46:31.997
So— We're good then? Okay, if you guys would get together and Brandon,

46:32.013 --> 46:35.234
let him know that These 2 will set up a meeting and see if

46:35.250 --> 46:38.430
we can move forward. I'm wondering if it's,

46:38.767 --> 46:42.123
uh, I'm wondering if October 20th is

46:42.155 --> 46:45.687
enough time. I think we should hold it off until it's

46:46.187 --> 46:48.834
ready. Yeah. Because, so, so go ahead and just put that as a future.

46:49.605 --> 46:53.234
There's nothing pressing other than it's on the list, and we know, I know we've

46:53.734 --> 46:56.943
been to some meetings. It's got— we need to move it forward, but let's give

46:57.443 --> 47:00.987
it some time. So Just tell David to get some meetings,

47:01.083 --> 47:04.564
and when it's ready, we'll bring it back. So, okay,

47:05.351 --> 47:08.126
then we'll hold that one off. Let's move on to— actually,

47:08.784 --> 47:12.137
Lacey, did you hear from— I was just reaching

47:12.233 --> 47:15.987
out. Lacey and I've been communicating back and forth on this, and we don't

47:16.003 --> 47:19.821
see the Giffords here to be able to talk to this,

47:19.950 --> 47:23.479
but the information

47:23.543 --> 47:26.528
is in the council's packet. Packet, I provided some context for the council,

47:27.154 --> 47:30.364
um, and I'm happy to maybe just at least represent

47:30.864 --> 47:34.120
the issue and see if there's any questions or concerns of whether the council has

47:34.168 --> 47:37.009
a desire or not to move forward with this,

47:37.828 --> 47:41.696
um, in next— because there's no, no actionable item this

47:42.196 --> 47:45.612
time. But long story short, um, the city of Eagle Mountain

47:46.112 --> 47:49.560
was approached by, um, Mr. and Mrs. Gifford, who are trained, uh,

47:49.640 --> 47:53.673
chaplains and have offered their services to the

47:54.173 --> 47:58.059
city of Eagle Mountain. In my last city, we had chaplain

47:58.123 --> 48:01.683
services for our police and our fire departments. It's another form

48:01.731 --> 48:04.820
of employee assistance program.

48:05.190 --> 48:09.033
It's kind of in times of crisis. And in fact, there was one particular

48:09.533 --> 48:13.280
week when in my last community,

48:14.184 --> 48:18.137
we had 3 baby deaths in a row that our EMTs and firefighters

48:18.313 --> 48:23.011
had responded to, and it was taking quite a toll on

48:23.059 --> 48:25.456
the mental state of our first responders.

48:26.858 --> 48:30.225
And so we had a chaplain that was just part of the organization,

48:30.338 --> 48:33.635
both for police and fire. They had an office. It was completely volunteer,

48:33.683 --> 48:38.508
no costs, no obligation, no proselyting, no— completely

48:38.798 --> 48:41.961
non-denominational and whatnot. Just a quick question.

48:42.330 --> 48:45.945
Just a resource not only to city

48:46.041 --> 48:49.528
personnel but also to victims

48:50.028 --> 48:53.706
and victim advocacy, members of the public if there's some sort

48:54.206 --> 48:57.627
of a traumatic event. And in order for someone to

48:58.127 --> 49:02.448
become a chaplain, they have to go through specific training and,

49:02.608 --> 49:06.127
and obtain that certification and, and also

49:06.627 --> 49:10.367
receive the endorsement from a municipality. So this is what their request was of

49:10.399 --> 49:14.476
the mayor. Um, uh, I am completely indifferent

49:14.976 --> 49:17.589
on the issue, just wanted to bring it to you for your consideration. And,

49:17.686 --> 49:20.446
um, if it's something the council would like to move forward with, we can put

49:20.946 --> 49:24.523
it on a future agenda. I only have one question. How do you determine who

49:25.023 --> 49:28.856
you want to use as your chaplain? Let's say that, uh, the Giffords

49:29.356 --> 49:33.029
are great, they move, or the Giffords aren't great and we

49:33.529 --> 49:36.867
want to find somebody else, or, you know, whatever the reasons are,

49:36.947 --> 49:40.255
how is that decision made? So, um, the way that

49:40.319 --> 49:44.269
it works is the certification program, the entity itself that does provide

49:44.769 --> 49:48.395
that, that mental health training that they've received their certification from,

49:48.909 --> 49:52.570
would be the responsible entity that would be providing that service for us.

49:53.325 --> 49:56.729
And when that question was asked of them, that mayor asked that exact same

49:56.777 --> 50:00.318
question, they did indicate in that room that they would want

50:00.415 --> 50:03.654
to try to bring others on board, at least 1

50:04.154 --> 50:07.476
or 2 or whatnot, so that they have some sort of succession planning and continuity.

50:09.306 --> 50:12.118
You know, because there's times when they might be out of town or something of

50:12.618 --> 50:16.311
that nature and other assistance might be needed. Does that require us doing an agreement

50:16.713 --> 50:19.983
with that organization so that the continuity

50:20.160 --> 50:23.735
is there? You know, I don't know.

50:23.816 --> 50:26.881
I don't— Honestly, I've never had an agreement

50:27.381 --> 50:31.141
before. It wasn't a formal interlocal or anything like that. It was something

50:31.641 --> 50:35.418
that was in establishment before I got there, and it's

50:35.918 --> 50:38.481
something I think evolved. I think it's very common back east,

50:38.578 --> 50:40.895
especially in fire and police situations.

50:43.177 --> 50:47.208
But you

50:47.708 --> 50:51.572
know, maybe if the council is inclined to do that, maybe. is something

50:51.604 --> 50:55.313
that you can assign as a mayoral responsibility that

50:55.377 --> 50:59.327
he could, you know, bring the next one on or something of that nature without

50:59.827 --> 51:02.860
necessarily having to have council action. Yeah, I just— I'm trying to walk through.

51:02.988 --> 51:06.296
I assume that we just need to recognize that there is a program.

51:06.440 --> 51:10.631
That's what I read. And then how do we institute

51:11.131 --> 51:15.320
it to begin and what the— I, I— it's

51:15.384 --> 51:18.981
a little more Like, I guess, understanding of

51:19.481 --> 51:22.192
how to help them to be successful in that. I understand what they do.

51:22.898 --> 51:26.190
How do we make sure that it's moving forward? And maybe

51:26.690 --> 51:30.525
you're right, maybe it is the mayor that kind of oversees that and reappointing them

51:31.025 --> 51:33.753
after a period of time, maybe if they're going to retire or whatever. It could

51:34.253 --> 51:37.205
be probably as simple as an appointment with the consent of council, something like that

51:37.705 --> 51:42.185
that we could put together. And, uh, and certainly we

51:42.685 --> 51:45.971
would want to make sure that contact information was available to the public if some

51:46.471 --> 51:49.333
kind of event occurred or whatnot, that they would have resources. Okay, thanks.

51:52.272 --> 51:55.696
Mayor, go ahead. Um, Ben, you mentioned that this

51:55.761 --> 51:59.360
is pretty common back east. Um, are there other

51:59.376 --> 52:02.683
cities in Utah that are doing this, and does ULCT

52:02.845 --> 52:05.910
have any resources to be able to address some of

52:06.410 --> 52:10.496
the concerns that Council Member Wood brought up, because I do think the continuity

52:10.512 --> 52:13.494
of service and letting people know that they exist,

52:13.639 --> 52:17.022
like, all of those things are important. Um, so I'm just

52:17.522 --> 52:20.935
wondering, do you know of more cities that have this? I don't

52:20.951 --> 52:24.078
know in Utah. Um, it's a good question. I can certainly put that out to

52:24.578 --> 52:28.472
the city managers group to see who, who might have one or, or more on

52:28.972 --> 52:31.808
board. Um, uh, and I'm,

52:31.872 --> 52:35.248
I'm not— it's never come up in the time that I've served as city manager

52:35.297 --> 52:36.904
with any of the league entities,

52:38.478 --> 52:40.539
you know, league meetings or anything that I've been involved in.

52:41.603 --> 52:44.669
Definitely it was something I saw growing up in New

52:45.169 --> 52:48.058
York, and it was something in Iowa as well. Thank you. Yep.

52:49.043 --> 52:52.653
Do the individual public safety organizations not have chaplains

52:52.669 --> 52:56.356
of their own? I thought that the Sheriff's Department and the

52:56.501 --> 52:59.721
