update. I'll keep it very high level, although if there's questions, let me

58:35.110 --> 58:37.717
know, and then we can just dive into the portfolio, if that works.

58:41.437 --> 58:44.733
Going into it, so, 5 things we're particularly looking

58:44.782 --> 58:49.030
at when we're thinking about investing in your portfolio. Is what

58:49.530 --> 58:52.534
the Fed's gonna do, inflation, growth, employment, and yields.

58:53.273 --> 58:56.005
Really, these are the 5 key things that we believe impact the portfolio,

58:57.290 --> 59:01.226
particularly what the Fed does with interest rates. So, hopping to

59:03.195 --> 59:06.615
what the market's expectation is for the Fed moving forward, whether we see

59:07.115 --> 59:10.545
interest rate hikes, cuts, or them holding. At the beginning

59:11.045 --> 59:14.682
of the year, you can see the market was predicting about 2 cuts

59:15.228 --> 59:18.889
throughout 2026, which are typically a quarter of a percent.

59:20.350 --> 59:23.690
That has since shifted, particularly around the time of the Iran war—

59:23.802 --> 59:27.817
or the Iran conflict starting, to where now the market

59:28.317 --> 59:32.328
is actually predicting that we may likely see 3 to 4 quarter-based—

59:32.828 --> 59:37.595
or quarter-percent hikes in the next 12 months. We have already seen one quarter-percent

59:37.611 --> 59:41.192
hike here in September. In the October meeting, the market does not

59:41.225 --> 59:45.063
expect another hike. However, in the December meeting, there is a higher probability.

59:45.658 --> 59:48.870
That being said, as the forecasts go out, we just take a little bit more

59:49.370 --> 59:52.227
of a— you take it with a grain of salt just because it's so far

59:52.727 --> 59:56.002
out. So much could change. Much of these interest

59:56.502 --> 01:00:00.226
rate hike decisions have been based on inflation, particularly because of the Iran

01:00:00.355 --> 01:00:03.695
war. We have seen a pretty big surge in oil prices.

01:00:04.193 --> 01:00:06.887
With the surge in oil prices, oil prices, we've started to see it trickle down

01:00:06.951 --> 01:00:10.316
into particularly gas and diesel.

01:00:10.460 --> 01:00:13.954
Obviously, a major part of our economy is transportation,

01:00:14.002 --> 01:00:17.944
so seeing gas and diesel go up can have trickle effects even further.

01:00:18.713 --> 01:00:22.222
And so to curb potential inflation, they've pushed

01:00:22.722 --> 01:00:26.405
into more of a hiking cycle, you could say. With that, you can

01:00:26.905 --> 01:00:30.251
look here, you can see, so the blue line is CPI going up

01:00:30.331 --> 01:00:34.259
above 3% again, and then below is a figure

01:00:34.759 --> 01:00:38.141
called Truflation. Essentially, it's a data scrape of more

01:00:38.641 --> 01:00:42.079
prices, still above 2%, the 2% target, but a little

01:00:42.579 --> 01:00:45.881
bit lower. What Kevin Warsh, the chairman of the Fed,

01:00:46.302 --> 01:00:49.556
has said is that really this is going to be a meeting-by-meeting

01:00:49.621 --> 01:00:53.352
decision. We, we don't expect it to be a hiking

01:00:53.401 --> 01:00:57.204
cycle quite like in 2022 where they jumped 500

01:00:57.558 --> 01:01:02.134
or 5%. This is likely going to be you

01:01:02.634 --> 01:01:05.051
know one or two cuts, and then see what they need to do in the

01:01:05.100 --> 01:01:09.028
future. So with that, we are still seeing pretty stable

01:01:09.961 --> 01:01:12.795
employment and growth in the economy.

01:01:13.342 --> 01:01:16.832
Consumers still are spending, and we're not seeing a major surge in unemployment.

01:01:17.448 --> 01:01:21.387
So with that, there really is no reason for the Fed to cut rates right

01:01:21.419 --> 01:01:24.636
now. If we started to see a little bit more of that, that would raise

01:01:24.668 --> 01:01:28.040
concern and maybe put the Fed on edge to either keep rates

01:01:28.540 --> 01:01:31.798
steady or potentially cut. But right now, the bigger picture is the

01:01:32.298 --> 01:01:35.587
inflation picture. So with that, it really does benefit the

01:01:36.087 --> 01:01:40.051
portfolio. We've seen yields come up over the last month to where this is

01:01:40.067 --> 01:01:43.712
covering the 2-year Treasury note. You can see we're up in about the

01:01:43.760 --> 01:01:47.726
4.9% range right now in 2026.

01:01:48.352 --> 01:01:51.945
This is really higher than we've been in a long time. So with your portfolio,

01:01:51.961 --> 01:01:55.507
it's a great opportunity because we're able to invest at these higher rates.

01:01:56.052 --> 01:01:59.823
That being said, I make the joke that what's good for your portfolio

01:02:00.323 --> 01:02:03.721
