01:08:50.760 --> 01:08:54.981
insulated as we're investing a little bit longer term. You're covered

01:08:55.481 --> 01:08:58.257
in that aspect, if that makes sense. I think you're— oh, sorry.

01:08:58.754 --> 01:09:02.351
I think you're doing a really good job of explaining what the money

01:09:02.544 --> 01:09:05.996
is and what it's doing. Okay. I think Councilmember Wright— Why

01:09:06.496 --> 01:09:10.090
does it exist? Yeah, Councilmember Wright and I, and I'm sure many taxpayers, are like,

01:09:10.252 --> 01:09:13.577
Where is this money coming from? What's the source of it? And then

01:09:13.625 --> 01:09:17.111
what does the city use it for? When do we spend it? What is it

01:09:17.239 --> 01:09:20.821
like? Tell us more, someone. A real simple explanation.

01:09:21.175 --> 01:09:24.805
Yeah. Yes. Okay. Maybe I'll jump in. Yeah, yeah, yeah. Those questions and whatnot.

01:09:25.014 --> 01:09:29.575
So revenues can come from various different sources, but the primary sources

01:09:29.672 --> 01:09:32.467
would be from taxes, from fees,

01:09:33.302 --> 01:09:36.505
and, and those, and firm user rates and

01:09:36.538 --> 01:09:37.963
whatnot. So basically,

01:09:39.564 --> 01:09:42.870
as far as the city is concerned, we need to have a certain amount of

01:09:43.370 --> 01:09:46.933
reserves as a rainy day fund. So the state of Utah allows us

01:09:46.965 --> 01:09:50.901
to have no more than 35% but no less than 5% of

01:09:51.014 --> 01:09:54.482
next year's projected revenues. And so we,

01:09:54.627 --> 01:09:57.354
we have those in place in case something happens.

01:09:57.854 --> 01:10:00.770
You want to have a certain amount of reserves that if we did not have

01:10:00.803 --> 01:10:04.511
operations If some catastrophe happened, you didn't have

01:10:05.011 --> 01:10:08.278
revenue coming in, you could maintain operations for a certain number of days throughout the

01:10:08.778 --> 01:10:12.638
year. At any given point in time, we also fall into

01:10:12.863 --> 01:10:16.870
different times of the year. So much of our tax resources come

01:10:16.934 --> 01:10:20.685
in, as far as like property taxes, all at one time. And,

01:10:20.862 --> 01:10:23.939
and so we might be up in a certain part of the year and down

01:10:24.439 --> 01:10:28.331
in another part of the year. So rather than stick those revenues in a shoebox,

01:10:28.813 --> 01:10:32.345
Right. We choose to invest them and get a return on the

01:10:32.426 --> 01:10:36.006
taxpayers' investments of user fees.

01:10:36.183 --> 01:10:40.101
Correct. And of taxes. I just think it's really important to understand what,

01:10:40.294 --> 01:10:44.324
what is there and why it's being generated. And there might be a capital project

01:10:44.824 --> 01:10:46.732
that's out there on the list that's going to be funded out of some of

01:10:47.232 --> 01:10:49.976
these funds, but it's not done yet and we haven't received a bill yet,

01:10:50.008 --> 01:10:52.432
so we can't pay it. So what are you doing with the resources in the

01:10:52.932 --> 01:10:55.921
meantime? I just think it's really important. important that people understand that, that these are

01:10:55.987 --> 01:10:59.451
not unallocated, right? They're not— they don't have a home.

01:11:00.087 --> 01:11:02.859
They're not extra, with the exception of the reserve.

01:11:04.990 --> 01:11:07.797
Anyway, and, and the reserve that we've chosen to keep is 35%,

01:11:08.153 --> 01:11:11.154
and we're struggling to do that this year with some of the tax issues that

01:11:11.654 --> 01:11:14.350
we're having. It's likely going to be less than that, just so people hear that

01:11:14.850 --> 01:11:18.337
and understand it. And even at a 35% reserve, we're only looking at

01:11:18.837 --> 01:11:22.085
a 6-month a 6-month window to be able to get from here to

01:11:22.101 --> 01:11:25.563
solving whatever problem we have with those reserves. So I just,

01:11:25.643 --> 01:11:28.705
I want the layperson, I want my daughter and my

01:11:29.205 --> 01:11:32.200
son who live here to understand that if they have questions. I don't want it

01:11:32.700 --> 01:11:36.480
to be hard for them to understand. Exactly right. And it's probably also

01:11:36.705 --> 01:11:40.055
important to understand that there are times when a city might take on

01:11:40.555 --> 01:11:43.454
a major project for which we might have a lot of bond proceeds that are

01:11:43.954 --> 01:11:46.807
sitting in the bank that we then have until anticipated costs that come out of

01:11:46.872 --> 01:11:49.988
that. So there's different times, not only

01:11:50.084 --> 01:11:53.698
in the cyclical nature of a city but also in a cyclical

01:11:54.198 --> 01:11:57.891
nature of a project. And then it's also important to note that we have many

01:11:57.955 --> 01:12:01.665
different buckets. So the general fund is different from the water fund

01:12:02.165 --> 01:12:04.846
and the sewer fund and, and that type of thing. And so we, we have—

01:12:05.826 --> 01:12:09.440
each of our enterprise funds has to work like a business and has to

01:12:09.940 --> 01:12:13.198
have its own reserves in place to be able to cover its own debt,

01:12:13.696 --> 01:12:17.503
debt coverage ratios and things like that. But it needs to operate

01:12:17.632 --> 01:12:21.342
so that it's not being— so we're not stealing money to come back into general

01:12:21.842 --> 01:12:24.779
fund, nor is it being subsidized by the general fund. It's being operated as a

01:12:25.279 --> 01:12:28.811
business entity. So there's lots of different reasons of how we get to where we

01:12:29.311 --> 01:12:32.313
get, but you're absolutely right, we are stewards of the taxpayer resources,

01:12:32.490 --> 01:12:36.361
and it's incumbent upon us to make sure that we utilize those resources to the

01:12:36.861 --> 01:12:39.760
best our ability, and any interest coming off of those can be used for the

01:12:40.260 --> 01:12:45.414
benefit of the residents we also serve. I couldn't have said it

01:12:45.914 --> 01:12:48.326
better. I have a couple questions. I was just going to ask, as far as

01:12:48.423 --> 01:12:51.869
you mentioned the Utah Money Management Act, what restrictions

01:12:52.241 --> 01:12:56.552
on investments must METAs abide by, such as equities,

01:12:56.649 --> 01:13:00.493
precious metals, et cetera? Could you tell us what confines you operate in?

01:13:00.927 --> 01:13:03.724
Yeah, absolutely. As far as what we're able to invest in,

01:13:04.820 --> 01:13:08.638
the vast majority of what we are able to invest in is essentially bonds or

01:13:10.120 --> 01:13:13.530
loan issuances from the U.S. government, from corporations,

01:13:13.595 --> 01:13:16.540
although corporations have a high A1 rating or higher.

01:13:17.268 --> 01:13:18.951
If you look at your asset allocation here,

01:13:20.588 --> 01:13:25.313
only about 23% is in corporate bonds. Everything else is more

01:13:26.328 --> 01:13:29.621
either implicitly or explicitly backed by the U.S. government.

01:13:29.670 --> 01:13:33.297
So certificates of deposit are only up to the $250,000,

01:13:34.272 --> 01:13:39.112
or under the Utah Money Management Act, it's $242,000

01:13:39.193 --> 01:13:42.210
maximum. And then U.S. agencies, U.S. Treasuries,

01:13:42.258 --> 01:13:47.391
and cash equivalents. So no equities, no gold, no— essentially

01:13:47.423 --> 01:13:51.055
a very safe bond portfolio

01:13:51.555 --> 01:13:54.758
is what we hold. So, and arguing this again, only out

01:13:55.258 --> 01:13:59.126
to about 5 years, specifically for managing both interest rates

01:13:59.495 --> 01:14:03.702
and liquidity. You, you do— we do want an ample amount of liquidity available

01:14:04.202 --> 01:14:06.705
to the city within a few-year time period. So,

01:14:07.700 --> 01:14:10.109
and then that's— Does that help answer the question? Yeah, okay, I think so.

