01:20:38.589 --> 01:20:42.379
what are we doing and what are you seeing other cities within Utah

01:20:42.879 --> 01:20:46.797
that are using METR in terms of an investment committee, investment policy statement?

01:20:47.343 --> 01:20:50.507
Where could we improve our practice?

01:20:51.037 --> 01:20:54.649
Yeah, yeah, great question. I think What we're seeing with other cities is actually

01:20:54.763 --> 01:20:58.168
very similar to how we work with Eagle Mountain. So obviously here we work

01:20:58.249 --> 01:21:02.626
with Kimberly and Erin, have either semi-annually

01:21:02.642 --> 01:21:06.023
or 3 times a year visits. I think with Eagle Mountain,

01:21:06.040 --> 01:21:09.841
actually, you're at a size now where we're having discussions about meeting 4

01:21:10.341 --> 01:21:12.620
times a year, whether that's in person or Teams,

01:21:13.314 --> 01:21:18.240
where we get a liquidity update, where we get a kind

01:21:18.740 --> 01:21:21.552
of what is the city spending in the next year, or 6 months to a

01:21:21.632 --> 01:21:26.155
year, even out to 2 years, just so that we can better plan the

01:21:26.443 --> 01:21:30.276
shorter end of the portfolio. From there, though, other cities—

01:21:30.308 --> 01:21:33.259
we do work with a couple other cities who have a finance committee that meet

01:21:33.759 --> 01:21:37.333
together. However, similar to Eagle Mountain,

01:21:37.349 --> 01:21:41.294
a lot of cities use us as more of a rainy day, longer-term investment

01:21:41.794 --> 01:21:45.915
fund. And so typically the finance committee discussions revolve around more

01:21:46.029 --> 01:21:48.743
of like what we're spending in the next 3 to 6 months.

01:21:50.170 --> 01:21:54.019
So METER, for most of our Utah municipalities, we don't

01:21:54.051 --> 01:21:57.466
join those meetings unless it's to do a presentation or

01:21:58.352 --> 01:22:01.648
if they are talking about longer-term reserves. Does that make

01:22:02.148 --> 01:22:05.983
sense? Does that help answer the question? Yeah, I think so. Are you seeing

01:22:06.177 --> 01:22:09.860
any cities— I'm just curious, how much are

01:22:09.910 --> 01:22:13.704
you managing of the city's total assets relative to

01:22:14.494 --> 01:22:17.925
PTIF? Are you seeing more money move your way out of

01:22:18.182 --> 01:22:22.001
PTIF? From what I can

01:22:22.501 --> 01:22:25.085
find, it sounds like there's only 4 or 5 cities in the state that are

01:22:25.554 --> 01:22:28.702
breaking outside of the state portfolio. Just curious

01:22:29.202 --> 01:22:33.711
what you're seeing as a trend. For the most part, I'd say

01:22:34.211 --> 01:22:36.632
we have about half of our cities that use us more as a rainy day

01:22:37.132 --> 01:22:40.525
fund that keep us at about 50% of their total reserves

01:22:41.025 --> 01:22:44.532
or less. Other cities use us up to about 80% of their

01:22:44.757 --> 01:22:47.979
reserves. We at METER

01:22:48.508 --> 01:22:51.698
have decided we don't recommend giving

01:22:51.730 --> 01:22:55.305
us more than 80% of your reserves to manage, simply because

01:22:55.578 --> 01:22:59.169
it— you know, we could, we would just front-load it,

01:22:59.185 --> 01:23:02.641
but there's better options. We believe the PTIF for the cash portion could

01:23:03.141 --> 01:23:06.948
be much better. So we have a maximum that we usually top

01:23:07.448 --> 01:23:10.220
out at about 80%. Okay. But I'd say it's about half that use us in

01:23:10.252 --> 01:23:13.413
that function, and the other half use us just under 50%. Okay.

01:23:13.947 --> 01:23:18.799
Thank you. And I guess here, I think it's about 30% that

01:23:18.815 --> 01:23:22.618
