Marcus, is they would have to be able to gravity feed to that

03:41:42.053 --> 03:41:45.780
existing lift station or else they

03:41:46.280 --> 03:41:49.300
can't connect? Yeah, or they're going to have to come back and ask for permission

03:41:49.800 --> 03:41:50.082
to do something different.

03:41:55.928 --> 03:41:59.216
That might solve my concern. We were under the impression of

03:41:59.232 --> 03:42:02.519
that. We were never planning to— I mean, there's sewer already to our property.

03:42:03.028 --> 03:42:06.603
through the additional stuff with QTS that was to a lift station. And so

03:42:07.099 --> 03:42:10.706
it was never even a consideration on our end to do something like

03:42:11.206 --> 03:42:14.185
that. Can you tell we love lift stations? I love lift stations. No, I— this—

03:42:14.393 --> 03:42:17.070
I really wanted to say to everyone, I know— I love them. I love them

03:42:17.570 --> 03:42:19.939
so much I want to get rid of them all. That I'm taking a lot

03:42:20.439 --> 03:42:23.995
of your time, but this is almost more beneficial as a conversation for me than

03:42:24.495 --> 03:42:26.736
it might even be for you guys, which I appreciate. That's how much I'm invested

03:42:27.236 --> 03:42:30.424
in this going the right way for the city. I I'm

03:42:30.924 --> 03:42:33.619
grateful for Marcus too. He's been really good to sit with us and do it

03:42:34.119 --> 03:42:37.939
the right way. Okay, so, uh, Councilmember Wood, I appreciate

03:42:37.955 --> 03:42:39.978
you being patient. I know you wanted to jump in a while ago. It's kind

03:42:40.478 --> 03:42:44.169
of been on this end. Did you still— Yeah, so the other question is,

03:42:44.233 --> 03:42:47.187
why are we giving them 20 years when we don't have a TIF that we're

03:42:47.687 --> 03:42:50.029
looking at? We don't have a CRA. Why do they need 20 years for a

03:42:50.529 --> 03:42:54.686
development agreement? Um, we— I don't

03:42:54.750 --> 03:42:57.600
think— I'm not wedded to 20 years. 20 years. I just want to make sure

03:42:58.100 --> 03:43:00.970
it has time to develop. If they think they can develop it faster than that,

03:43:01.180 --> 03:43:03.319
then I'll let them decide how long the agreement needs to be.

03:43:04.240 --> 03:43:07.560
As I look at this, it's 40 acres. I mean, it's a

03:43:08.060 --> 03:43:11.419
pretty small facility. Yeah, I mean, if they can do it in 10

03:43:11.919 --> 03:43:14.570
years, great. Why not? Let's change it to 10. Why not do benchmark chunks,

03:43:14.720 --> 03:43:17.620
then make them come back and ask for more time? I would rather do that

03:43:18.120 --> 03:43:21.202
than have them do nothing for 15 years. So they already have to start Vertical

03:43:21.702 --> 03:43:25.212
in 3. Why would we go more than— They don't lose their development rights.

03:43:25.548 --> 03:43:29.173
So 20 years just has it sit there for 20 years. Again,

03:43:30.247 --> 03:43:33.872
I'm not wedded to 20 years. That was just the number we picked. I would

03:43:34.372 --> 03:43:36.598
say I'm not comfortable with 20 years. There's no reason to give it to them.

03:43:36.743 --> 03:43:39.966
There's no benefit to the city for 20 years. So I think that needs to

03:43:40.466 --> 03:43:42.388
be amended, but I don't know what that timeframe should be.

03:43:43.655 --> 03:43:47.350
With the user, I mean, they're building data centers You know,

03:43:47.850 --> 03:43:50.700
less than 5 right now. Yeah, so if they have to start in 3,

03:43:50.890 --> 03:43:53.670
wouldn't at least 10 be plenty? That's what I'm getting at. So I mean,

03:43:54.170 --> 03:43:55.990
just— I'm trying to find— I'm saying why not set it at 6, and if

03:43:56.490 --> 03:43:59.870
they need more, let them come ask for it? That only gives

03:44:00.370 --> 03:44:02.910
them 3 to build. Isn't the point that we want them to get started and

03:44:03.410 --> 03:44:05.250
build it? I agree with you. I'm just saying that gives them 3. If they

03:44:05.750 --> 03:44:08.330
start in 3, is 3 enough? Depending on who it is, right?

03:44:08.760 --> 03:44:11.650
I've said 6. Give them 6 and make them come back and say we're wrong.

03:44:12.150 --> 03:44:14.220
Is there a consensus there? I just want to know, because if that's— when it

03:44:14.720 --> 03:44:18.140
comes back— That's my opinion. We haven't made a motion on this yet

03:44:18.640 --> 03:44:22.600
anyway. So you're talking about 6 years total for construction from the moment of start?

03:44:23.100 --> 03:44:25.860
Yeah. From the moment of starting. And if they need more time, they can come

03:44:26.360 --> 03:44:29.320
back and ask for an amendment. That's within the Master Development Agreement code anyway.

03:44:29.820 --> 03:44:32.980
So I would be curious for you to gather—

03:44:33.480 --> 03:44:36.820
maybe you have data on this— like how many megawatts would be needed to

03:44:37.320 --> 03:44:40.029
support the data center that would go there, because it might be a lot smaller

03:44:40.529 --> 03:44:43.739
than all of us are thinking. And we're getting our minds wrapped around, like,

03:44:44.028 --> 03:44:48.044
the energy source and maybe the— like, how many turbines would you need for natural

03:44:48.060 --> 03:44:51.320
gas? And should we go that direction? That's a great question, and I'd love to

03:44:51.336 --> 03:44:55.593
address it. We're looking at a lot of different options, and there are many

03:44:55.673 --> 03:44:59.785
options. On that site, you're not going to get anything— even with the greatest

03:45:00.285 --> 03:45:04.105
of everything on the planet to date, you're probably not getting more than 200 megawatts.

03:45:04.346 --> 03:45:07.397
on the lower end is kind of what maybe you're going to be looking at.

03:45:07.879 --> 03:45:11.588
To answer your question specifically, because it's a great question, I'm only concerned,

03:45:12.374 --> 03:45:15.473
only concerned about Layton. I'm honestly trying to do my best for them.

03:45:16.951 --> 03:45:20.451
I said 20 because I thought, what if this buyer

03:45:20.499 --> 03:45:23.550
comes and they're like, we're not ready yet, and we'll just let Layton stay there

03:45:24.050 --> 03:45:27.179
till they finish out QTS, which they're projected 8 or 9 years. So I thought,

03:45:27.227 --> 03:45:30.774
okay, worst case they go 10 years. then they start

03:45:30.790 --> 03:45:34.368
doing their stuff, they have 10 years. For me,

03:45:34.400 --> 03:45:38.363
it's not to have this sit for that. I mean, it's a fantastic concern,

03:45:38.379 --> 03:45:41.667
which I agree with. If Layton wasn't in the picture, and we're

03:45:42.167 --> 03:45:44.924
all agreeing it's Layton Construction, I can say, right? If Layton Construction wasn't in the

03:45:44.972 --> 03:45:48.052
picture using the land, which I told them,

03:45:48.165 --> 03:45:50.555
and I'm not going to go back on my word, I gave them my word,

03:45:50.651 --> 03:45:55.239
they have a year and a half left. So we're looking at about March

03:45:55.739 --> 03:45:59.223
of '28. My concern is if you say the development agreement was

03:45:59.255 --> 03:46:02.823
good for 4 years, a year and a half has already gone for

03:46:02.855 --> 03:46:05.120
anything that they may look to do. And I don't even know what that might

03:46:05.620 --> 03:46:08.848
look like. Just blanket statement of saying, I,

03:46:09.202 --> 03:46:12.303
I want to take care of anybody that I said can use that

03:46:12.351 --> 03:46:16.256
land, and I'd hate to come back and tell a buyer sorry,

03:46:16.754 --> 03:46:20.289
or tell Layton get out of here because I only have so long with the

03:46:20.789 --> 03:46:23.887
city. So I do Completely understand your concern,

03:46:24.401 --> 03:46:28.255
and I don't— I think it's very valid. Maybe 20 years isn't the mark.

03:46:28.576 --> 03:46:31.771
The only reason I said that was if in case they needed it.

03:46:32.413 --> 03:46:35.576
I would say to that, Regan, that then you're probably not ready for a development

03:46:36.076 --> 03:46:38.980
agreement. If you're coming in and asking for a start time and you're not ready

03:46:39.012 --> 03:46:42.304
to execute that, why ask now? Because you

03:46:42.804 --> 03:46:45.997
have obligations to the current user that you want to keep. You're a year and

03:46:46.497 --> 03:46:49.847
a half too early. Per se, I agree, but not— and I'd also

03:46:49.911 --> 03:46:52.844
disagree because let's say I sold it tomorrow.

03:46:53.454 --> 03:46:56.099
You're going to be 6 to 8 months of due diligence on that buyer.

03:46:56.804 --> 03:47:00.203
So that puts me now, I'm only essentially 7 months away from,

03:47:00.588 --> 03:47:03.794
if they wanted to start, right? 7 months away from Layton

03:47:04.294 --> 03:47:07.721
being done. The reason we brought it to City Council now is because we think

03:47:08.221 --> 03:47:11.361
we're at the exact right time. Some of these companies are going out a year

03:47:11.409 --> 03:47:13.879
for due diligence, and I don't blame them. They're looking at spending a lot of

03:47:14.379 --> 03:47:18.246
money and a lot of time and a lot of resources. So even if I

03:47:18.746 --> 03:47:22.389
sold it in an hour, I will promise you I'm 6 to 8

03:47:22.889 --> 03:47:26.821
months on the company's due diligence based on conversations I've had months

03:47:27.321 --> 03:47:30.337
prior to coming here. And so I didn't do it in the spring when we

03:47:30.837 --> 03:47:34.126
first came because I agreed with you, we were 2 years too early. But your

03:47:34.626 --> 03:47:37.739
ability to hit that 3-year mark that's in your development agreement is pretty low.

03:47:38.686 --> 03:47:42.476
I think that I would hope for you to— I don't know what goes

03:47:42.976 --> 03:47:44.740
in with the amendment. I don't think that's a bad idea, because I think if

03:47:44.965 --> 03:47:48.160
a developer or a builder came to you and said, hey, these were the

03:47:48.660 --> 03:47:51.917
parameters, I think you guys are upstanding men that would say, yeah, you're not trying

03:47:51.949 --> 03:47:54.631
to pull the wool over our eyes. I would agree. That's why I'm saying I

03:47:54.663 --> 03:47:58.147
would— starting in 3 years is— that doesn't bother me.

03:47:58.452 --> 03:48:01.358
I just want to give them time to do what they needed to do.

03:48:01.503 --> 03:48:03.815
And if there is a provision where they can come back and say,

03:48:04.987 --> 03:48:08.525
these are the legitimate reasons why, I would

03:48:09.025 --> 03:48:11.098
agree with you. I wouldn't want somebody to come in there and be like,

03:48:11.114 --> 03:48:13.751
guess what, we can do whatever we want for 30 years. I agree.

03:48:14.507 --> 03:48:17.410
I would just hope that there's some clear language for the end user to say,

03:48:18.039 --> 03:48:21.274
yeah, if you need more time, come to the city and explain why.

03:48:21.322 --> 03:48:24.929
And one of those reasons might be— So on that one,

03:48:25.041 --> 03:48:28.663
Marcus, it looks like if we can— I didn't get an exact consensus,

03:48:28.744 --> 03:48:31.532
but I want to keep moving forward. But we need to tighten up the timing

03:48:31.740 --> 03:48:35.586
somewhere. I've heard 6, 10-ish. Would you

03:48:35.714 --> 03:48:39.913
consider 6 after the 3-year or whatever

03:48:39.977 --> 03:48:43.294
that trigger is? So then it gets them closer to 10,

03:48:43.823 --> 03:48:47.108
which I think is where I would be. But I'm also sensitive

03:48:47.608 --> 03:48:52.461
to like the reasons that you're giving. But would you consider 6

03:48:52.961 --> 03:48:56.468
after the 3? To Rich's point, I'm after a year and a half. That's to

03:48:56.968 --> 03:48:59.134
whatever, you know what I mean? If you said, hey, 3 years after the year

03:48:59.634 --> 03:49:03.598
and a half to start and then give them time, that's fine. I'm not worried

03:49:04.416 --> 03:49:06.664
for how— because they, like you said, I think it's a great point, they can

03:49:07.164 --> 03:49:10.357
come back to you and say we need more time. I just— I want— So

03:49:10.373 --> 03:49:14.163
based on the comments that we've had, just clean up

03:49:14.211 --> 03:49:17.438
the timing that we've heard. I think that Brett made the point

03:49:17.938 --> 03:49:20.505
that I agree with. I think it needs to come back to a work session.

03:49:20.569 --> 03:49:23.427
I think we've talked We've talked about a number of the things. I think Marcus

03:49:23.460 --> 03:49:27.153
has at least some parameters of things that need to be talked about,

03:49:27.779 --> 03:49:30.846
but we're not there on this. Well, and that's why I'm trying to

03:49:31.346 --> 03:49:33.961
just say clean up the timing and move on. So is there any other points

03:49:34.461 --> 03:49:37.638
that need to be cleaned up specifically? Not really?

03:49:38.296 --> 03:49:41.042
So the ones we've talked about, if we can clean those up, then we can

03:49:41.542 --> 03:49:45.457
go to the negotiation of the fine details if we need to. Is that where

03:49:45.957 --> 03:49:49.148
we're at, you think? Yeah. Okay,

03:49:50.046 --> 03:49:53.239
so if that's where we're at on this one, then let's jump back to

03:49:53.383 --> 03:49:56.849
14— Hold on, let's make

03:49:57.349 --> 03:49:59.640
a vote before we pass this and then have to come back to it again

03:50:00.140 --> 03:50:03.362
and make another vote. If we're gonna table it, let's table it and then go

03:50:03.571 --> 03:50:06.844
on to the other one. We're going back to 14A, the rezone.

03:50:07.421 --> 03:50:10.999
That's what I'm saying. We haven't tabled— there's no vote that's taken place on 14B.

03:50:11.448 --> 03:50:15.121
There's no vote on 14A. Right. So does it matter

03:50:15.621 --> 03:50:19.098
what order? We moved our discussion to 14B. So he's

03:50:19.146 --> 03:50:22.594
saying we moved it, we moved on to 14B, so resolve that before going back

03:50:23.094 --> 03:50:26.780
to— It is resolved. Well, but he said, he said take a vote on 14B.

03:50:27.550 --> 03:50:30.693
Yes, let's do a motion and then go back

03:50:31.193 --> 03:50:34.510
to 14A. So, uh, Mayor, I, I move

03:50:34.542 --> 03:50:38.391
that City Council table, uh, agenda item 14B.

03:50:39.772 --> 03:50:43.353
Second. Okay, we have a motion by Councilmember Whiting and a second

03:50:43.853 --> 03:50:46.692
by Councilmember Wright. We'll start the roll with Councilmember Clark. Yes.

03:50:47.062 --> 03:50:49.390
Councilmember Wright. Yes. Councilmember Whiting. Yes.

03:50:49.711 --> 03:50:53.019
Councilmember Wood. No. Councilmember Huish.

03:50:54.351 --> 03:50:57.771
No. That passes 3-2. All right,

03:50:57.900 --> 03:51:01.464
now go back to 14A.

03:51:07.017 --> 03:51:10.256
Mayor, I make a motion that we table item 14A.

03:51:10.982 --> 03:51:11.626
Second.

03:51:15.990 --> 03:51:18.907
All right, we have a motion by Councilmember Wright and second by Councilmember Whiting.

03:51:19.280 --> 03:51:21.954
We'll start the vote with Councilmember Wright. Yes.

03:51:22.181 --> 03:51:24.648
Councilmember Whiting. Yes. Councilmember Wood. Yes.

03:51:25.250 --> 03:51:27.724
Councilmember Hewish. Yes.

03:51:28.033 --> 03:51:31.368
Councilmember Clark. Yes. Okay, and that passes. Can I just

03:51:31.868 --> 03:51:35.351
make a comment? This is all moving in a forward direction.

03:51:35.400 --> 03:51:38.901
No, this is great. Like, even though it feels like nothing happened. Hey,

03:51:38.917 --> 03:51:42.210
this is great. I think a lot went in the direction you really wanted it

03:51:42.710 --> 03:51:44.620
to go. And it helped me understand a lot. I appreciate you guys taking the

03:51:45.120 --> 03:51:47.993
time and talking me through a lot of things. Anything needed from me based on

03:51:48.493 --> 03:51:51.768
tonight, or will they— will work just behind the scenes, correct? Okay,

03:51:51.928 --> 03:51:55.768
have a great night. Thank you. All right,

03:51:55.784 --> 03:51:59.230
15A is also a public hearing. It's Ordinance

03:51:59.730 --> 03:52:03.158
of Eagle Mountain City, Utah, updating the Culinary Water Impact Fee Facilities

03:52:03.271 --> 03:52:06.715
Plan. And we have our city engineer here to give

03:52:06.748 --> 03:52:07.478
the presentation.

03:52:18.297 --> 03:52:20.708
Good evening, Mayor and Council. Good to be with you tonight.

03:52:24.064 --> 03:52:26.418
Get this pulled up real quick and then we can move ahead.

03:52:33.740 --> 03:52:38.378
All right. Discussion tonight is on the culinary water IFFP

03:52:38.700 --> 03:52:42.838
update. As you guys know, this came before you approximately

03:52:42.871 --> 03:52:45.684
a year ago as a new IFFP.

03:52:48.459 --> 03:52:49.418
At that point,

03:52:52.019 --> 03:52:55.536
as you know, the city is growing fast. I don't

03:52:55.601 --> 03:52:59.621
have to tell you guys that. And it's changing a lot. And so to

03:52:59.637 --> 03:53:02.414
keep up with that, we're bringing it back to you a year after it was

03:53:02.914 --> 03:53:06.108
approved. And there are reasons for that. And we'll go through those reasons in just

03:53:10.051 --> 03:53:13.817
One of the biggest reasons is new development,

03:53:14.765 --> 03:53:17.912
the Spring Run area, Talons Cove area. You guys have

03:53:18.412 --> 03:53:21.862
seen this concept plan come before you. As you know,

03:53:22.151 --> 03:53:26.165
there's actually been just east of the Walmart

03:53:26.665 --> 03:53:29.665
area developments that have come in already, ready to develop right now.

03:53:30.340 --> 03:53:33.503
It was originally assumed that this piece of property or

03:53:34.003 --> 03:53:36.634
this big chunk of land in our city was going to be out in the

03:53:37.134 --> 03:53:40.071
future, you know, 20, 30 years. But as we can see,

03:53:40.088 --> 03:53:42.967
it's, it's moving ahead faster than we originally anticipated.

03:53:43.549 --> 03:53:46.850
And so we wanted to add this section of land to our

03:53:46.947 --> 03:53:48.957
culinary water IFFP at this time.

03:53:50.431 --> 03:53:52.944
Um, just, just a note on the IFFP,

03:53:53.782 --> 03:53:57.366
uh, for the public mostly, but it, it's a plan that is

03:53:57.703 --> 03:54:01.159
really focused on the next 5 years of projects that we

03:54:01.659 --> 03:54:04.750
have going on. And And what it is

03:54:04.815 --> 03:54:07.792
is trying to nail down what those costs are going to be so that we

03:54:08.292 --> 03:54:11.563
can get impact fees, um, as development comes in.

03:54:12.469 --> 03:54:15.703
We don't want to put the cost of that new growth on the

03:54:16.203 --> 03:54:19.745
existing, uh, members of the community. We want

03:54:20.245 --> 03:54:23.955
to put that on the members who are moving into the community, in the—

03:54:23.971 --> 03:54:27.872
into the community. So the

03:54:28.372 --> 03:54:31.676
point of the IFFP is to nail down a cost of what that impact is

03:54:31.692 --> 03:54:34.717
going to be in the next 5 years and then move ahead with it.

03:54:37.270 --> 03:54:37.780
So, um,

03:54:42.209 --> 03:54:45.063
another thing that we kind of nailed down— we,

03:54:45.355 --> 03:54:48.810
we got a lot more information. We've completed a 30% design

03:54:49.572 --> 03:54:53.459
on our, uh, Central Utah Waterline

03:54:53.556 --> 03:54:56.186
that's going to parallel SR 73.

03:54:57.420 --> 03:55:00.867
Um, as you can see from this map, it's just— it's

03:55:01.367 --> 03:55:04.586
on the north side. We haven't nailed down the alignment exactly, but what

03:55:05.086 --> 03:55:08.850
we did nail down was the cost. Um, and we were trying to more

03:55:08.898 --> 03:55:12.890
than anything get the cost nailed down so that we can figure out bonding and

03:55:13.390 --> 03:55:17.443
funding and things moving forward. And so as we got that nailed down, we actually

03:55:17.459 --> 03:55:20.553
went back and compared it. Uh, it was very different from what

03:55:20.585 --> 03:55:24.242
was in the original IFFP, so we needed to update that cost.

03:55:28.701 --> 03:55:32.492
And then the cost of diesel is up, the cost

03:55:32.992 --> 03:55:36.688
of construction is up. As you know, costs are rising

03:55:36.802 --> 03:55:40.288
daily, and because of that, we went through

03:55:40.722 --> 03:55:43.903
all of the cost estimates in the IFFP from last year

03:55:44.032 --> 03:55:47.398
and just made sure that we're actually covering all the costs that

03:55:47.898 --> 03:55:51.038
the— that is— that these capital projects are

03:55:51.538 --> 03:55:51.605
going to take.

03:55:56.125 --> 03:56:00.046
So bottom line, um, our cost from the 2025 plan

03:56:00.143 --> 03:56:03.437
for the next 5 years for all of our capital

03:56:03.614 --> 03:56:07.118
projects for culinary water, uh, was $73.2 million.

03:56:07.857 --> 03:56:11.618
We had to bump that up to $109.1

03:56:11.650 --> 03:56:11.939
million.

03:56:15.166 --> 03:56:18.595
So big jump. Uh, part of that— and

03:56:18.611 --> 03:56:22.328
I should go back to the SR-73 project to explain— that

03:56:22.376 --> 03:56:25.789
project went up quite a bit in the cost, and that's kind of the majority

03:56:25.965 --> 03:56:29.330
of the, the cost difference that you're seeing. Part of that is

03:56:29.346 --> 03:56:32.647
because through the 30% design process, we were

03:56:33.336 --> 03:56:36.765
looking at it going, this will cover our water needs

03:56:37.214 --> 03:56:39.955
for maybe the next 10 years, but we want to look further than that.

03:56:40.084 --> 03:56:43.280
We want to upsize this pipe and make sure it covers

03:56:43.377 --> 03:56:46.977
