04:18:56.471 --> 04:19:00.046
an ordinance of Eagle Mountain City amending the Eagle Mountain Municipal

04:19:00.062 --> 04:19:02.370
Code 17100-040,

04:19:03.108 --> 04:19:06.378
master site plans. Back to Rob. Thank you, Mayor.

04:19:06.507 --> 04:19:10.659
Excuse me, my apologies for the formatting error I just observed with

04:19:11.159 --> 04:19:14.989
the auto reformat that did with my paragraphs and didn't catch it. My only consolation

04:19:15.489 --> 04:19:18.198
is that you see even in national news stories sometimes errors that the editors miss.

04:19:19.321 --> 04:19:23.316
This is an attempt to do a couple of things. One is clarify or make,

04:19:24.519 --> 04:19:28.706
you know, a little easier to read perhaps some of the content, but mainly it's

04:19:29.206 --> 04:19:31.867
to get away from the question like we had with the charter school not that

04:19:31.915 --> 04:19:35.733
long ago as to what to do when your code now says,

04:19:35.846 --> 04:19:39.441
well, you need a master site plan for that project, because you've

04:19:39.941 --> 04:19:41.512
got more than 5 acres you're dealing with. And then, oh, by the way,

04:19:41.528 --> 04:19:44.354
you need a regular site plan approval thereafter.

04:19:44.900 --> 04:19:48.384
That makes sense for when you've got a configuration of multi-pad sites or

04:19:48.448 --> 04:19:52.190
lots or maybe a multi-tenant building setup. But in

04:19:52.302 --> 04:19:54.775
that particular instance, it created a little bit of confusion if we're trying to go

04:19:54.791 --> 04:19:58.195
right by the letter of the law. So this attempts to make that clear that

04:19:58.741 --> 04:20:02.337
it's one or the other, and there are some set conditions which follow what's basically

04:20:02.837 --> 04:20:06.177
in the code now. Thank you. Maybe reword it a little bit. The other one

04:20:06.677 --> 04:20:09.214
though, that's more of a policy shift, which I try to draw your attention to

04:20:09.471 --> 04:20:13.183
in the staff report, is what you see in D and E on this slide.

04:20:13.488 --> 04:20:16.975
And this is a little bit of a different take on how we process our

04:20:17.475 --> 04:20:20.622
site plans and plats together. Current code basically says, well,

04:20:21.538 --> 04:20:24.189
where they're required, you got to bring in your site plan before you do your

04:20:24.689 --> 04:20:28.222
plat. Just today and previously, which is what

04:20:28.270 --> 04:20:31.415
raised the question in my We had parties

04:20:31.915 --> 04:20:34.766
that were saying, well, what if we just want to divide a big property we

04:20:35.266 --> 04:20:38.613
have? We'll follow the platting standards, we'll take care of the

04:20:39.113 --> 04:20:42.541
road planning, offsite and on, the utilities, et cetera, but we don't

04:20:43.041 --> 04:20:46.981
have a user yet. When we get one, then sure, they can come in and

04:20:47.481 --> 04:20:51.197
apply for your site design approval and get

04:20:51.697 --> 04:20:55.189
their architectural stuff pinned down, the parking and landscaping, et cetera, at that time.

04:20:56.023 --> 04:20:59.965
For each of the properties that they're trying to work

04:21:00.013 --> 04:21:03.698
with. And so that's not an unusual

04:21:04.198 --> 04:21:07.368
request. It's actually pretty common from experience in other jurisdictions that,

04:21:07.881 --> 04:21:11.775
yeah, someone could, with residential or commercial or industrial and so forth,

04:21:11.951 --> 04:21:15.156
ask for that. The idea though would be that we would not offer that opportunity

04:21:15.656 --> 04:21:18.922
for residential. It'd only be for commercial and industrial if you're agreeable

04:21:19.422 --> 04:21:22.165
with what's written there in particularly E.

04:21:26.347 --> 04:21:30.044
Questions? So just a

04:21:30.544 --> 04:21:34.102
couple questions about this. Uh, preliminary plat

04:21:34.602 --> 04:21:37.435
approval vests in, uh, uses,

04:21:37.790 --> 04:21:40.943
correct? Once a preliminary plat has been approved?

04:21:41.691 --> 04:21:44.474
I'm sorry, let's— to Marcus.

04:21:46.934 --> 04:21:50.941
So, um, Site plans are often

04:21:50.957 --> 04:21:54.716
after preliminary plat approval, but a site plan can act as the preliminary

04:21:54.748 --> 04:21:57.206
plat. So I guess it varies.

04:21:59.550 --> 04:22:03.374
But the preliminary plat is the binding document? They're both

04:22:03.569 --> 04:22:04.839
binding land use approvals.

04:22:07.224 --> 04:22:11.094
Now, they vest different things. The preliminary plat

04:22:11.175 --> 04:22:14.405
typically will vest certain things like lot layouts and road

04:22:14.905 --> 04:22:18.483
widths and all that. A site plan will vest additional things.

04:22:19.466 --> 04:22:23.507
But again, the site plan can be the preliminary plat. So— Yeah.

04:22:25.757 --> 04:22:29.179
Mayor, apologies. I wanted to point— The site plan is generally

04:22:29.211 --> 04:22:33.090
more specific than a preliminary plat. With our OpenGov system,

04:22:33.252 --> 04:22:37.680
staff agreed anyway, with that new program, it would be cleaner for us to

04:22:38.180 --> 04:22:39.765
have— even though you can combine the two,

04:22:40.897 --> 04:22:44.663
paperwork-wise, registry-wise, et cetera, if you will, to have the 2

04:22:44.679 --> 04:22:47.868
applications be separate. They can still come in, be reviewed

04:22:48.368 --> 04:22:51.890
together like they are now, but the record would show

04:22:52.114 --> 04:22:54.710
this is the application for the site plan, this is the one for the plat.

04:22:55.399 --> 04:22:59.229
They can be linked, associated with each other, but then you don't have the confusion

04:22:59.598 --> 04:23:02.354
issue that goes with having them combined in that system. Plus,

04:23:03.748 --> 04:23:07.763
sometimes we'll get— we've gotten documents

04:23:08.263 --> 04:23:09.883
that are supposed to be part of— I remember this happening,

04:23:11.456 --> 04:23:14.199
happening, but I can't remember the project. But the documents for the plat came in

04:23:14.699 --> 04:23:17.959
with the site plan. But there was such a mix there

04:23:18.459 --> 04:23:20.799
that it was hard to tell, well, this is— we've got enough for the plat

04:23:20.977 --> 04:23:23.329
without a lot of extra work to sort through it all, and this is for

04:23:23.829 --> 04:23:26.963
the site plan. It's much cleaner just to have this is that and this is

04:23:26.995 --> 04:23:31.187
this. So my concern is this

04:23:31.461 --> 04:23:35.144
enables the developers to play the game. With us. And I,

04:23:35.433 --> 04:23:38.533
I think we've already had this discussion a number of times in the last year

04:23:39.224 --> 04:23:42.566
about what does the rest of the plan look like, what is the road

04:23:42.582 --> 04:23:46.630
flow, what does all those things look like. But if we, if we give

04:23:47.130 --> 04:23:50.084
them the preliminary plat, that requirement is no longer required.

04:23:50.710 --> 04:23:54.502
They now can, can do their particular plat without

04:23:54.550 --> 04:23:58.518
having to contemplate how, how the road flows. And for me,

04:23:59.208 --> 04:24:03.162
I would prefer have master site plan Every time, because that requires

04:24:03.662 --> 04:24:06.222
them to show the full layout, phasing, all of those types of things,

04:24:06.740 --> 04:24:09.897
and then we can come back and have a chat about your plat. That's my

04:24:22.260 --> 04:24:25.214
I'm going to let that marinate for a minute while I see if anyone came

04:24:26.005 --> 04:24:29.294
to speak up. Okay, all right then. In that case,

04:24:29.653 --> 04:24:33.146
at 9:50, I will open the public hearing.

04:24:34.808 --> 04:24:37.479
Not a chance, no one. All right,

04:24:38.369 --> 04:24:41.779
anyone here that came to speak on this item? Seeing no one,

04:24:42.961 --> 04:24:46.267
I'll close the public hearing at 9:50 and bring it back to council and see

04:24:46.767 --> 04:24:48.952
if they had enough time to, uh— go ahead.

04:24:50.000 --> 04:24:52.688
So, Councilmember Wood, I just want to ask you a couple questions. I mean,

04:24:53.363 --> 04:24:56.958
Help me understand. I think I understand what

04:24:57.458 --> 04:25:01.067
you're saying, but can you just kind of give me maybe a broader picture

04:25:01.567 --> 04:25:04.967
view of that? And how would you— how does

04:25:05.015 --> 04:25:08.080
it relate to this application? How would you solve this application?

04:25:08.594 --> 04:25:11.306
Are you looking at this as something that needs to be denied?

04:25:12.767 --> 04:25:16.234
I don't like putting the preliminary plat first

04:25:16.250 --> 04:25:20.697
or separating them. I think the two have to be contemplate, especially with the decisions

04:25:20.729 --> 04:25:23.924
we've been making lately, we're still asking for them to

04:25:24.424 --> 04:25:28.500
come and show us. So rather than changing the language where they can put

04:25:29.000 --> 04:25:31.567
one or the other first, where it's not really clear which one has to go

04:25:31.631 --> 04:25:35.003
first, they're always going to do the preliminary plat first because they want

04:25:35.503 --> 04:25:39.001
to build a drive-through. They don't

04:25:39.033 --> 04:25:42.228
want to come and talk to you about how that traffic plan is going to

04:25:42.728 --> 04:25:46.273
contemplate through their entire development. How it's going to connect to the other developments

04:25:46.773 --> 04:25:50.850
adjacent to it. They want to build their drive-through property.

04:25:51.933 --> 04:25:55.513
And I think that there's a lot of negative that ends up happening with that

04:25:55.562 --> 04:25:58.190
where we didn't have an opportunity to go,

04:25:58.352 --> 04:26:00.088
that's going to cause these other problems.

04:26:01.778 --> 04:26:05.025
To me, good planning means let's see the whole picture,

04:26:05.411 --> 04:26:08.240
then we'll come back and say, yeah, we're okay with that use for that particular

04:26:08.740 --> 04:26:11.710
thing. But the master site plan, you have If they don't know that in advance,

04:26:12.242 --> 04:26:15.389
then they aren't really planning out the site. They're just trying to make a buck

04:26:15.889 --> 04:26:19.749
on a piece of land. I'm not okay with that. Okay. So you're

04:26:20.249 --> 04:26:23.734
just— you don't like paragraph E? Correct. You're okay

04:26:24.234 --> 04:26:24.612
with paragraph D?

04:26:29.987 --> 04:26:33.970
Being concurrently submitted, sure, because you got to still evaluate

04:26:34.470 --> 04:26:37.928
the master, but Yeah, the preliminary plat preceding the master site plan.

04:26:38.121 --> 04:26:41.621
I've been no on that for since I was on the Planning

04:26:41.637 --> 04:26:45.555
Commission, 'cause it's created additional problems. And we've had many

04:26:45.651 --> 04:26:49.103
developers come and do that very, very thing to us. And they do it whether

04:26:49.603 --> 04:26:53.037
you're talking about commercial or residential, which they're smart, by the way.

04:26:53.278 --> 04:26:56.409
I would do the same thing. But it's not in the best interest of

04:26:56.909 --> 04:26:59.604
the city to let them do that, 'cause it's exactly what Brett said. It piecemeals

04:26:59.717 --> 04:27:03.983
it. Okay, so you don't like

04:27:04.483 --> 04:27:06.267
paragraph D either, either of those paragraphs?

04:27:09.589 --> 04:27:12.986
They can be— I mean, they can be reviewed together anyway. We were doing that

04:27:13.486 --> 04:27:17.159
before. I don't like putting preliminary plat before master site plan

04:27:17.693 --> 04:27:21.140
or allowing the preliminary plat to be approved without the master site plan.

04:27:22.376 --> 04:27:24.606
So not a big fan of the language.

04:27:33.251 --> 04:27:37.283
All right. If there's no other questions or comments— oh, go ahead.

04:27:38.031 --> 04:27:38.193
Yeah,

04:27:42.170 --> 04:27:46.311
I'll tell you what I've seen in my career is I've seen plats come first,

04:27:46.375 --> 04:27:49.614
honestly, and that the development community actually appreciates the

04:27:50.114 --> 04:27:54.062
ability to create lots of land because Because that's what— all

04:27:54.562 --> 04:27:59.629
you're doing is creating lots and laying out roads. You're not— they

04:28:00.210 --> 04:28:03.755
still have to come in for a site plan. If you look at

04:28:04.255 --> 04:28:08.635
the requirements for— if you look at the requirements in '16

04:28:08.651 --> 04:28:11.584
for a preliminary plat, it requires a traffic plan.

04:28:11.648 --> 04:28:15.390
It's a traffic report that shows anticipating

04:28:15.422 --> 04:28:20.164
trip generation and levels of service. But then you get into the site

04:28:20.664 --> 04:28:23.244
site plan, then you have to do a traffic impact study, which is a— and

04:28:23.744 --> 04:28:26.601
correct me if I'm wrong, but it's a higher-level study.

04:28:26.713 --> 04:28:30.407
So they build on each other. You create

04:28:30.907 --> 04:28:34.711
the lots, and then you come back with the site plan and

04:28:35.370 --> 04:28:38.534
give more detail. And you can

04:28:38.582 --> 04:28:42.484
ask Rob, he's talked to developers, and developers will

04:28:42.533 --> 04:28:45.439
tell you that they appreciate the ability late,

04:28:45.504 --> 04:28:48.249
just simply lay out lots and then be able to come in and give you

04:28:48.749 --> 04:28:52.007
some specificity when— after they've been

04:28:52.507 --> 04:28:55.908
able to at least lay out the lots. That doesn't

04:28:55.924 --> 04:29:00.179
give them the ability to come in and start laying out

04:29:00.822 --> 04:29:04.579
shopping centers. They can't do that without a site plan. So I

04:29:04.611 --> 04:29:09.283
just want to give you a little context from what I've seen throughout my career.

04:29:09.348 --> 04:29:12.303
Thank you. And then the other issue,

04:29:13.679 --> 04:29:16.666
again today, 200-acre or so parcel,

04:29:17.754 --> 04:29:20.530
property owners are interested in trying to rezone it.

04:29:21.800 --> 04:29:25.125
The way our rezone and MDP codes work, you know, that pushes

04:29:25.625 --> 04:29:29.208
them into an MDP. But they right now are

04:29:30.129 --> 04:29:33.046
wanting to leave it as a blank slate. And then at some juncture when it's

04:29:33.546 --> 04:29:36.557
ready to plat and/or get a site plan approval, et cetera, then whoever comes to

04:29:36.573 --> 04:29:39.482
buy it, speculative will take care of those details,

04:29:39.530 --> 04:29:43.360
whether it's one party or multiple parties. That's— they

04:29:43.376 --> 04:29:46.806
want that freedom, and it's been— That's by design, though, Rob. I mean, you're literally

04:29:46.822 --> 04:29:50.364
talking to the people who wrote that code. We changed it from how it used

04:29:50.864 --> 04:29:54.419
to be because all people were doing was coming in, titling land, and then sitting

04:29:54.451 --> 04:29:57.641
on it for 8 to 10 years. And then 8 to 10 years from then,

04:29:57.657 --> 04:30:00.461
we're going to go take a look at code that nobody's okay with.

04:30:01.038 --> 04:30:04.677
