that to go to nowhere. So that's also important that that happens.

04:38:11.300 --> 04:38:14.173
So, so it's a big win for the city. So glad to see that happen.

04:38:15.740 --> 04:38:19.272
Moving on to the speed study. So tonight we have Hales Engineering to

04:38:19.320 --> 04:38:21.481
present the results of the city citywide speed study.

04:38:22.348 --> 04:38:25.912
This came about— we evaluated 35 locations that were identified by

04:38:26.201 --> 04:38:30.119
the mayor and council members and residents, and Hales has gone out,

04:38:30.135 --> 04:38:33.684
collected data, assessed road conditions, and developed recommendations

04:38:33.748 --> 04:38:37.008
for speed limits and potential traffic calming measures.

04:38:37.746 --> 04:38:40.974
Our goal tonight is for Hales to present their data,

04:38:41.937 --> 04:38:45.405
review their recommendations, and then receive direction from you guys on how to

04:38:45.469 --> 04:38:48.820
proceed. Now, we see this as an ongoing process rather than

04:38:49.320 --> 04:38:53.483
just a one-time study. Since the study, we've been collecting more information from residents,

04:38:54.364 --> 04:38:57.473
Utah County sheriffs as well, and this is something we want to continue

04:38:57.569 --> 04:39:01.143
on a regular basis. In addition, we also want to

04:39:01.175 --> 04:39:04.396
revisit the locations where we implement traffic calming

04:39:04.896 --> 04:39:07.489
measures to see, are they effective and how effective are they?

04:39:08.082 --> 04:39:11.126
So with that time, I'm going to turn it over to Hales Engineering to walk

04:39:11.626 --> 04:39:11.815
through the results.

04:39:21.731 --> 04:39:25.032
Thank you, David. My name is Joseph Browning.

04:39:25.880 --> 04:39:29.276
And I'm Sean Elliott. And I don't know if you heard this,

04:39:29.293 --> 04:39:30.452
but we're looking for a traffic engineer.

04:39:35.190 --> 04:39:36.509
I'm a planner, though.

04:39:41.604 --> 04:39:45.082
Notice. Okay. You probably know what

04:39:45.259 --> 04:39:48.995
we're working on. Yeah. So, so I have to tell you, I came to

04:39:49.043 --> 04:39:51.925
Hales a year and a half ago. I worked for MAG for 29 years.

04:39:52.360 --> 04:39:56.787
I know all this stuff, all this stuff you're talking about, and I'm grateful

04:39:57.287 --> 04:40:00.620
we're talking about local transportation and not regional tonight because we'd be here

04:40:01.120 --> 04:40:04.367
for breakfast. So, and Sean, I didn't even

04:40:04.383 --> 04:40:07.818
recognize you. You look great. You just look awesome.

04:40:08.366 --> 04:40:10.914
Getting out of government service has probably been very good for you.

04:40:13.620 --> 04:40:14.821
The stress is gone.

04:40:17.840 --> 04:40:19.234
I didn't have— I could sleep.

04:40:22.636 --> 04:40:25.946
All right. Okay, so tonight

04:40:26.446 --> 04:40:28.999
we'll go through a bit of an introduction to the study.

04:40:29.779 --> 04:40:32.960
And we'll talk about some context about how speed

04:40:33.460 --> 04:40:33.561
limits are set.

04:40:36.998 --> 04:40:40.900
We'll talk about the locations that we've analyzed. We'll talk about— we've got

04:40:41.400 --> 04:40:45.212
a few proposed speed limit changes. And then we'll dive into

04:40:45.554 --> 04:40:49.304
traffic calming measures, different types of traffic calming measures that we've recommended.

04:40:50.117 --> 04:40:53.552
And then where those— and then

04:40:53.699 --> 04:40:54.497
where we recommend those.

04:40:58.189 --> 04:41:01.581
Okay, so we, as David said,

04:41:01.710 --> 04:41:04.925
we looked at 35 different segments in the city

04:41:05.668 --> 04:41:08.994
to evaluate the posted speed limits and the

04:41:09.494 --> 04:41:12.729
speeds on those roads and to look at whether traffic calming measures

04:41:12.827 --> 04:41:16.605
are needed. And so we

04:41:17.105 --> 04:41:22.182
came back and about half of them ended up Warranting

04:41:22.280 --> 04:41:23.246
traffic calming measures,

04:41:25.828 --> 04:41:29.046
locations that we recommended them. Some of

04:41:29.546 --> 04:41:32.405
them have a greater need than others, and we'll get into that.

04:41:36.350 --> 04:41:39.691
So what we typically take a look at when we

04:41:40.191 --> 04:41:42.887
do a speed study is we want to look at vehicle speeds.

04:41:45.040 --> 04:41:48.790
We laid out tubes at each of these locations. which collect

04:41:49.290 --> 04:41:52.916
vehicle traffic, like traffic volumes,

04:41:53.786 --> 04:41:55.929
vehicle speeds, and vehicle classification.

04:41:57.487 --> 04:42:00.994
We had these tubes set out for 2 consecutive

04:42:01.043 --> 04:42:04.307
weekdays at each location, which is more than we typically do,

04:42:05.126 --> 04:42:08.730
just so that we have a solid picture of

04:42:10.854 --> 04:42:14.748
the— Of how fast people are driving. We want

04:42:15.248 --> 04:42:19.168
to look at, as part of that, a crucial number

04:42:19.668 --> 04:42:24.933
is the 85th percentile speed. So that's the speed at which 85%

04:42:25.191 --> 04:42:27.555
of cars are traveling below that speed.

04:42:31.572 --> 04:42:35.211
We try to plan for these thresholds because it represents

04:42:35.597 --> 04:42:38.399
the speed that the vast majority of drivers are going at or below.

04:42:40.773 --> 04:42:44.161
And we also do a review of the safety, the crash

04:42:44.661 --> 04:42:47.838
data. We take the 5 most recent years of crash data and we

04:42:47.998 --> 04:42:52.349
take a special look at speed-related

04:42:52.849 --> 04:42:55.609
crashes, especially severe speed-related crashes.

04:42:56.588 --> 04:43:00.747
And we dive into as well crashes

04:43:00.843 --> 04:43:04.440
involving non-motorized users, so pedestrians and cyclists, because they are

04:43:04.536 --> 04:43:05.661
more vulnerable.

04:43:07.669 --> 04:43:11.092
And speed, the speed of

04:43:11.124 --> 04:43:13.631
a vehicle in a collision affects them the most greatly.

04:43:16.272 --> 04:43:19.807
We also look at the context surrounding the

04:43:20.307 --> 04:43:24.068
area. So the land uses, the access frequency,

04:43:24.972 --> 04:43:28.650
the density of driveways. We also take

04:43:29.150 --> 04:43:32.251
a look at whether We see much pedestrian

04:43:32.299 --> 04:43:34.156
or cyclist activity in the area.

04:43:35.432 --> 04:43:38.968
And we look at growth and development in the area. And to this I

04:43:39.000 --> 04:43:42.853
would add the functional classification of roadways. So whether it's a

04:43:43.353 --> 04:43:44.497
local road, a collector, or an arterial,

04:43:46.645 --> 04:43:50.572
in terms of what the road is designed to accommodate,

04:43:51.910 --> 04:43:54.687
whether it's designed more for mobility or for access,

04:43:54.704 --> 04:43:58.068
we take that into consideration. And then the roadway design.

04:43:58.440 --> 04:44:01.758
So if there are areas with limited sight distance, for instance,

04:44:04.234 --> 04:44:07.752
Pony Express has some areas with a

04:44:07.801 --> 04:44:11.388
35-mile-an-hour speed limit with some vertical and horizontal curves.

04:44:11.484 --> 04:44:15.883
And we wouldn't necessarily want to recommend raising the speed limit there because

04:44:16.964 --> 04:44:20.449
you can't really see— you can't see

04:44:20.949 --> 04:44:22.934
far enough for a higher speed to be appropriate.

04:44:25.891 --> 04:44:28.438
And the roadway condition, we take that into account.

04:44:29.217 --> 04:44:32.072
And of course, the existing speed limit.

04:44:36.625 --> 04:44:38.595
So this is a map of all the locations that we studied.

04:44:43.050 --> 04:44:46.542
These were— we coordinated with the city, with city staff

04:44:46.623 --> 04:44:50.462
in determining Where these data

04:44:50.962 --> 04:44:55.776
collection points are. So the circles, the dots represent the location

04:44:55.809 --> 04:45:00.422
where the data collection actually occurred, and then the lines

04:45:00.922 --> 04:45:04.304
beyond that are the broader segments between major intersections that

04:45:04.336 --> 04:45:04.819
we looked at.

04:45:09.410 --> 04:45:11.337
So how speed limits are set,

04:45:12.872 --> 04:45:15.504
guidance on this is has changed in recent years.

04:45:17.680 --> 04:45:21.033
Conventional wisdom had been to align speed

04:45:21.533 --> 04:45:24.646
limits with the speeds of the vehicles that they're traveling.

04:45:26.480 --> 04:45:30.523
But recent industry guidance and based on research

04:45:31.186 --> 04:45:34.776
has been that context should be a

04:45:34.824 --> 04:45:37.450
more important factor in determining the speed limit.

04:45:39.870 --> 04:45:42.731
Recently, UDOT has updated its policy to reflect this,

04:45:43.825 --> 04:45:47.040
and since implementing that policy, they've lowered the speed

04:45:47.540 --> 04:45:51.277
limit on quite a few roads. In fact, I believe they may have lowered it

04:45:51.342 --> 04:45:54.005
on SR 73 in recent years.

04:45:58.668 --> 04:46:01.704
We want to look at those factors I mentioned earlier.

04:46:03.405 --> 04:46:06.822
That's the driving factor. The speed limit,

04:46:08.259 --> 04:46:11.842
and so the goal is still alignment.

04:46:12.536 --> 04:46:15.990
We do want vehicle speeds and the posted speed limit to match as close as

04:46:16.123 --> 04:46:20.125
we can. In instances where we feel

04:46:20.625 --> 04:46:23.664
like a lower speed limit is more appropriate, in that case,

04:46:23.825 --> 04:46:27.578
we'd recommend traffic calming measures to aid in the or

04:46:28.520 --> 04:46:31.706
if needed, roadway design features to. To help

04:46:32.206 --> 04:46:35.542
bring those vehicle speeds into alignment with the appropriate posted

04:46:35.558 --> 04:46:35.865
speed.

04:46:42.751 --> 04:46:46.465
When a speed limit is changed, it doesn't—

04:46:47.092 --> 04:46:51.986
what we found is that it typically doesn't have a huge effect on

04:46:52.019 --> 04:46:55.986
vehicle speeds. So if

04:46:56.486 --> 04:46:59.372
a speed limit is lowered, for instance, We'd want to make sure that we implement

04:46:59.404 --> 04:47:02.766
traffic calming measures to go along with that,

04:47:03.265 --> 04:47:06.497
to make sure that people are driving at the

04:47:06.997 --> 04:47:07.189
appropriate speed.

04:47:10.459 --> 04:47:13.471
Enforcement is certainly an important part of this,

04:47:14.244 --> 04:47:20.088
you know, of alignment, but targeted

04:47:20.281 --> 04:47:23.630
enforcement, if— Unless

04:47:24.130 --> 04:47:28.099
it's kept up over time, once the campaign

04:47:28.375 --> 04:47:28.975
has ended,

04:47:31.864 --> 04:47:35.649
the effect wears off. So we want to— so we're

04:47:35.794 --> 04:47:39.564
taking a careful look at design features, at traffic calming,

04:47:40.015 --> 04:47:43.974
to make sure that these effects are more permanent

04:47:44.055 --> 04:47:47.433
beyond just the lowering of the speed limit. Can I ask a question?

04:47:48.713 --> 04:47:52.656
Can I just add too that the bottom quote there that talks about,

04:47:54.110 --> 04:47:56.502
you know, raising or lowering your speed limit,

04:47:58.356 --> 04:48:01.581
you know, when you look at the 85th percentile and you look at the context

04:48:02.081 --> 04:48:05.323
and you say this road really should function at let's say 30 miles an hour,

04:48:05.469 --> 04:48:07.146
right?

04:48:11.605 --> 04:48:14.701
You— there's always that fear that if you raise the speed limit, then people are

04:48:15.201 --> 04:48:18.653
going to go even faster. Studies have shown, data has shown that's not

04:48:18.701 --> 04:48:22.093
true. People drive what they feel is safe for that road.

04:48:22.578 --> 04:48:25.824
And it's how you design the road and the characteristics along that

04:48:25.873 --> 04:48:30.815
road that encourage people to either go faster or go slower. And so

04:48:30.977 --> 04:48:34.235
going and having a study on a corridor and saying it should be

04:48:34.735 --> 04:48:35.418
raised or it should be lowered,

04:48:38.380 --> 04:48:42.611
you know, people aren't going to speed just because a new sign went

04:48:43.111 --> 04:48:43.930
up, they're not going to slow down because you've lowered the speed limit either.

04:48:44.188 --> 04:48:47.703
You've got to do other things too if they feel already comfortable driving

04:48:47.719 --> 04:48:50.850
that speed. So yeah, yep, when it's appropriate,

04:48:52.497 --> 04:48:56.339
I would like to ask questions about how we can mitigate concerns

04:48:56.759 --> 04:49:00.522
for kids crossing streets there, because on the one that you did

04:49:00.619 --> 04:49:03.817
suggest to raise, there's 2 schools worth of

04:49:03.849 --> 04:49:08.558
kids that are crossing at different points on it, and I just want

04:49:09.058 --> 04:49:10.248
to figure out like school zone or what. So I want to come back to

04:49:10.748 --> 04:49:12.134
that. When we get to those, we'll go through them.

04:49:15.759 --> 04:49:19.551
Yeah. Okay, and this is a map showing where we've

04:49:19.567 --> 04:49:23.534
recommended speed limit changes. The red indicates

04:49:24.034 --> 04:49:27.132
a lower speed limit and the blue indicates a raising of the speed limit.

04:49:27.810 --> 04:49:30.827
And then the green area along Golden Eagle,

04:49:30.859 --> 04:49:34.418
there is no posted speed limit along that particular section.

04:49:34.614 --> 04:49:37.015
And so we've recommended a Haste speed limit for that.

04:49:40.450 --> 04:49:44.021
So diving into the details for some of our justifications on this.

04:49:46.130 --> 04:49:49.343
So Silver Lake Parkway is currently posted at 35.

04:49:51.705 --> 04:49:54.982
The speed limit signs are up at the north end by

04:49:55.482 --> 04:49:59.865
Pony Express. And There

04:49:59.913 --> 04:50:03.537
is no signage between the

04:50:04.037 --> 04:50:07.763
roundabout and the southern terminus,

04:50:07.844 --> 04:50:10.996
but it is assumed that it is still 35.

04:50:12.052 --> 04:50:16.111
Given the school

04:50:16.144 --> 04:50:19.778
there, the high amount of pedestrian

04:50:20.278 --> 04:50:24.040
activity, especially the children, we recommended

04:50:26.061 --> 04:50:29.296
And the traffic congestion there. We've recommended lowering the speed limit

04:50:29.796 --> 04:50:33.482
there to 30. On Golden Eagle Road,

04:50:33.498 --> 04:50:35.380
where there is no posted speed limit there,

04:50:37.020 --> 04:50:40.122
we've recommended that the speed limit there also be set to 30.

04:50:41.560 --> 04:50:44.791
Mm-hmm. And that is because 30 miles per hour is

04:50:45.291 --> 04:50:48.637
appropriate for a collector-type road with limited access.

04:50:50.783 --> 04:50:54.294
And we wouldn't recommend a higher speed limit because of that similar

04:50:54.794 --> 04:50:58.554
pedestrian activity there. Porter's Crossing Parkway,

04:50:59.247 --> 04:51:02.967
this is a location where we did recommend raising the speed

04:51:03.467 --> 04:51:05.028
limit to 30. It's currently posted at 25.

04:51:06.978 --> 04:51:10.099
For similar reasons, it's a collector road

04:51:10.327 --> 04:51:11.416
with very limited access.

04:51:13.990 --> 04:51:17.848
However, to your point, we did recommend some traffic calming

04:51:17.880 --> 04:51:20.315
measures there, some pedestrian safety enhancements,

04:51:23.246 --> 04:51:26.930
because of the— in part because of the crash history that's there.

04:51:29.213 --> 04:51:31.112
But we'll talk about that in a bit.

04:51:32.837 --> 04:51:36.218
And then Airport Road, we've— it's currently

04:51:36.250 --> 04:51:39.421
posted at 45. We've recommended lowering that to 40.

04:51:40.521 --> 04:51:44.195
And there is— at least within the developed area.

04:51:45.221 --> 04:51:48.379
And the reason for that is because the road north

04:51:48.879 --> 04:51:52.354
of there is— there's a lack of development that

04:51:52.854 --> 04:51:55.945
the higher speed makes sense there, but we felt that a lower speed limit was

04:51:56.041 --> 04:52:00.064
more appropriate for the developed portion, especially where there's a crosswalk.

04:52:01.090 --> 04:52:04.521
I acknowledge that there is a school zone which does

04:52:04.617 --> 04:52:08.063
handle the before and after school periods of children

04:52:08.563 --> 04:52:10.992
crossing there, but— That's not a school zone. Pedestrians are also able to use that

04:52:11.056 --> 04:52:14.621
crosswalk outside of that school period. So that's

04:52:15.589 --> 04:52:18.326
another factor into why we recommend a lowering there.

04:52:19.692 --> 04:52:23.581
And then last location with the change, we'd recommended that

04:52:23.950 --> 04:52:27.588
Pole Canyon Boulevard, east of Raccoon Lane, so think

04:52:28.088 --> 04:52:31.162
about Lake Mountain Road,

04:52:32.181 --> 04:52:35.513
we recommended lowering the speed limit to

04:52:35.658 --> 04:52:38.909
30. 30 on either side of Lake Mountain Road, at least for the

04:52:39.409 --> 04:52:41.198
portion where it's a narrow 2-lane road.

04:52:43.589 --> 04:52:46.686
And pedestrian crash history is

04:52:46.735 --> 04:52:48.779
part of the reason for that. It's a narrower road.

04:52:50.009 --> 04:52:53.468
There aren't any pedestrian facilities on

04:52:53.485 --> 04:52:57.561
the west side. And then we did notice some pedestrian activity,

04:52:59.518 --> 04:53:02.594
even independent of the charter school there, on the east side. on the east side.

04:53:03.750 --> 04:53:07.895
There's also the— there's a large drop

04:53:07.976 --> 04:53:13.257
in the road grade that

04:53:16.222 --> 04:53:19.654
we feel we wouldn't want any

04:53:19.863 --> 04:53:23.425
speed higher than a 30-mile-an-hour limit for

04:53:23.925 --> 04:53:24.005
that, so. Thank you.

04:53:29.317 --> 04:53:32.975
Okay, so that's the speed limit proposed changes.

04:53:33.071 --> 04:53:36.585
There weren't many of them, but, um, and again, I think as Joseph said,

04:53:36.922 --> 04:53:40.163
um, you know, looking at the context of the roads, you know,

04:53:40.387 --> 04:53:44.206
Golden Eagle, it was designed as a collector road, it's functioning

04:53:44.222 --> 04:53:47.832
as a collector road, but there's 2 schools on it, and so there's got to

04:53:48.332 --> 04:53:52.019
be some balance, right? And so this portion I'm going to go through just some

04:53:52.519 --> 04:53:55.349
of the mitigation issues that we're thinking of.

04:53:56.427 --> 04:53:59.788
Like I said earlier, you can change the speed limit, but if— especially if

04:54:00.288 --> 04:54:02.844
you're trying to lower the speed limit, you've got to put some things in to

04:54:03.344 --> 04:54:05.547
try to slow people down. It isn't going to happen just by signage.

04:54:07.063 --> 04:54:10.856
So the way that we looked at this is anything where speeds

04:54:10.905 --> 04:54:14.280
were— the 85th percentile speed was 5 miles an

04:54:14.780 --> 04:54:19.478
hour or less over the posted speed limit, we're not proposing changes

04:54:19.527 --> 04:54:23.148
on though. I think there's one. Generally. Yeah, generally. Yeah, and I'll go through

04:54:23.648 --> 04:54:27.234
those. But for the most part, you know, we see that being close

04:54:27.734 --> 04:54:31.144
enough to the posted speed limit to not see

04:54:31.160 --> 04:54:34.173
that there's, there's a warrant to put in the cost on those.

04:54:35.070 --> 04:54:38.756
That and looking at crash rates and so forth also. And I think

04:54:39.256 --> 04:54:42.025
the few that we do propose, there's because there are some higher crash rates.

04:54:43.243 --> 04:54:46.400
The 5 to 10 miles over the 85th percentile.

04:54:48.446 --> 04:54:52.409
Those are the ones that we have suggestions for that we would call moderate

04:54:52.909 --> 04:54:56.564
areas. And then the 10 miles over, and it's one

04:54:56.596 --> 04:55:00.116
area of town, you probably all saw it, but those are the

04:55:00.616 --> 04:55:05.371
ones that we have to have a pretty good discussion on. So let's

04:55:05.871 --> 04:55:09.662
see. So there's 2 ways to slow traffic. There's the easy ways,

04:55:10.096 --> 04:55:13.437
the perceptual, the the way they look at things. A lot of

04:55:13.937 --> 04:55:17.104
that can be handled with signage, with markings, with feedback signs,

04:55:17.201 --> 04:55:20.366
you know, the speed limit, here's what you're going, that kind

04:55:20.866 --> 04:55:24.950
of thing. They're usually quick, low cost, so forth. Physical measures,

04:55:25.771 --> 04:55:28.039
call them vertical, so speed bumps,

04:55:30.030 --> 04:55:32.615
that kind of thing, islands, bulb-outs,

04:55:33.639 --> 04:55:36.419
and I'll get into each one of those in a minute. But those are more

04:55:36.484 --> 04:55:39.710
costly. They're more permanent. They come

04:55:39.742 --> 04:55:43.158
with maintenance costs too. So, and then at the bottom here,

04:55:43.206 --> 04:55:46.670
combining multiple speed management measures is usually more

04:55:47.170 --> 04:55:51.064
successful. So one area I noticed in town, you mentioned

04:55:51.564 --> 04:55:55.201
it earlier, it's Ida Hodges

04:55:55.378 --> 04:55:58.842
Parkway. Ida Hodges. Yeah. I've noticed there where

04:55:59.342 --> 04:56:02.883
you've done the lane, the funneling with the white

04:56:03.092 --> 04:56:06.386
Curb lanes on each side, right? And that tends to slow traffic

04:56:06.886 --> 04:56:10.339
down, right? But in that case, on that road being such

04:56:10.839 --> 04:56:14.822
a— I would call it a— it's not wide enough really

04:56:15.322 --> 04:56:17.682
to be a collector, but it's being used as a collector to get around,

04:56:17.778 --> 04:56:21.249
right? So probably you need a few other things in

04:56:21.749 --> 04:56:25.678
there to help out that too. OK, so some

04:56:26.178 --> 04:56:28.738
of the types of calming techniques. We have speed cushions.

04:56:29.142 --> 04:56:32.801
You'll notice these are— they're basically a speed

04:56:33.861 --> 04:56:37.279
hump, but they cut out 2 places for fire

04:56:37.407 --> 04:56:40.762
trucks to be able to go down the middle and not have to be impeded

04:56:41.262 --> 04:56:44.774
as they are going to a fire. One downside in Eagle Mountain

04:56:45.274 --> 04:56:47.807
is that works for your fire trucks, but I guess it would not work for

04:56:47.839 --> 04:56:51.819
your ambulances or your police cars. And so— Does

04:56:51.932 --> 04:56:54.901
it work for school buses? Does it work for school buses? Are they about the

04:56:54.933 --> 04:56:58.642
same size? Same track size? I don't know. I would imagine

04:56:58.674 --> 04:57:01.356
they're similar, but I think we would need to look into that.

04:57:02.752 --> 04:57:06.060
Our hope would be speed buses would probably— they're not in

04:57:06.560 --> 04:57:09.962
need to get somewhere in a hurry, right, like the emergency services.

04:57:10.235 --> 04:57:13.382
So what about normal cars, normal trucks?

04:57:14.393 --> 04:57:17.862
Their wheelbase is narrow enough that it would require to go over— they couldn't just

04:57:18.362 --> 04:57:21.563
line up those and just fly right over? Right. The upside is you

04:57:22.063 --> 04:57:25.192
can line up and at least get one tire through, which is what we all

04:57:25.692 --> 04:57:28.772
do, right? So this is if your emergency

04:57:29.272 --> 04:57:32.627
services are concerned about the speed

04:57:33.127 --> 04:57:35.999
humps slowing them down getting places, or speed tables.

04:57:37.680 --> 04:57:41.244
When we talked with your staff, it wasn't as much of a concern,

04:57:41.357 --> 04:57:45.100
so because it would only be impactful to the fire

04:57:45.165 --> 04:57:49.194
engines and I mean, not impactful. Uh, the ambulances,

04:57:49.306 --> 04:57:52.527
it wouldn't work. The police cars, it wouldn't work. They'd still have to slow down

04:57:52.591 --> 04:57:56.277
to go over these. So, um, if you had ambulances that

04:57:56.777 --> 04:58:00.812
could maneuver this, then it would be even a, a more strong

04:58:01.068 --> 04:58:03.792
option for the city, I guess, is, is what I'm saying. So,

04:58:04.834 --> 04:58:08.007
um, there's also speed tables. Uh,

04:58:08.456 --> 04:58:11.404
these are good usually at crosswalks. They're the ones where you,

04:58:11.661 --> 04:58:13.952
you go up and then you sort of level out for a bit, and then

04:58:13.968 --> 04:58:17.718
you go back down. So a little bit longer. They're not as—

04:58:19.112 --> 04:58:22.718
how would you put it? Severe? Damaging to the vehicle if

04:58:23.218 --> 04:58:26.259
they accidentally hit it going too fast, right? But they do get people to slow

04:58:26.759 --> 04:58:30.458
down. And they also are very good at crosswalks because they mark the

04:58:30.474 --> 04:58:34.144
fact that this is a crosswalk, right? People pay attention

04:58:34.160 --> 04:58:39.756
that it's there. So curb extensions, they're also called These

04:58:40.350 --> 04:58:43.690
work very well in pedestrian safety in that one,

04:58:44.268 --> 04:58:47.817
they make it so that the cross— the pedestrian has less road to

04:58:48.317 --> 04:58:51.606
have to cross over, for one. They also allow the pedestrian to

04:58:51.654 --> 04:58:55.106
stand out closer to where the drivers can see them. If there's

04:58:55.122 --> 04:58:58.318
parked cars that block where the pedestrian is, they can't be seen. And so—

04:58:58.735 --> 04:59:02.059
and they also allow drivers to feel a little bit more

04:59:02.107 --> 04:59:05.868
narrow, and therefore they helps slow drivers down. So probably

04:59:05.884 --> 04:59:08.166
one of the better options for you.

04:59:09.800 --> 04:59:13.216
A lateral shift. This is showing, and I hope

04:59:13.716 --> 04:59:17.692
you can see this, but basically what you're doing is with paint,

04:59:17.789 --> 04:59:20.929
and also there are some islands in here in the middle of the road and

04:59:21.010 --> 04:59:24.237
even on the shoulder to sort of aim cars to go do

04:59:24.253 --> 04:59:27.515
a curvature as they could drive down the road. We're not talking Lombard Street in

04:59:28.015 --> 04:59:30.513
San Francisco, but It's enough to get people to slow down.

04:59:31.493 --> 04:59:34.112
When I talk about Golden Eagle Way or Road,

04:59:35.349 --> 04:59:38.838
that's one place where we suggest these. And there's a straightaway on that

04:59:38.950 --> 04:59:42.249
road where you have the highest speeds. And the

04:59:42.749 --> 04:59:46.127
lower speeds are where the road is bending. So this would be one

04:59:46.627 --> 04:59:49.381
of those applications. Landscaping.

04:59:49.784 --> 04:59:53.075
So a great example in Eagle Mountain, in fact, you've got a lot of streets

04:59:53.575 --> 04:59:57.262
this way, but Silver Lake Parkway.

04:59:58.289 --> 05:00:02.046
On the diagonal portion of Silver Lake, it's sort of got a bend to

05:00:02.062 --> 05:00:05.964
it, but you also have a lot of trees, and that breaks down the visual

05:00:06.093 --> 05:00:09.545
view of drivers. Now, you could argue it makes it less safe

05:00:10.045 --> 05:00:13.848
for pedestrians because there's more things in the way, but in

05:00:14.105 --> 05:00:17.461
this case, I just drove it today. The speed limit's

05:00:17.573 --> 05:00:21.540
35, and I felt comfortable. Thank you. people around 33.

05:00:22.376 --> 05:00:25.381
And part of that's the bend, and part of that's just I can't see very

05:00:25.881 --> 05:00:29.335
far, right? And so landscaping can be

05:00:30.270 --> 05:00:32.656
an item to help break down speeds.

05:00:33.881 --> 05:00:37.476
Curb gutter sidewalk, of course, that's another way when you have a road that's not

05:00:37.588 --> 05:00:41.183
very well defined, defining it can

05:00:41.199 --> 05:00:44.545
help bring down speeds. But it also

05:00:44.561 --> 05:00:48.387
gives you a safe place for your pedestrians. pedestrians. The radar

05:00:48.887 --> 05:00:52.237
feedback signs, there's sort of mixed opinions on these.

05:00:53.320 --> 05:00:56.326
They seem to work better in residential streets than, say,

05:00:56.488 --> 05:00:59.883
Pony Express Parkway or major

05:01:00.383 --> 05:01:04.021
roads. They sort of blend into the background after a while with that much traffic

05:01:04.521 --> 05:01:08.709
driving on it. Also, they work better— you

05:01:09.209 --> 05:01:13.229
can choose to put them in permanent locations, but Sometimes moving them around is another

05:01:13.729 --> 05:01:16.890
way to get people to notice that, oh yeah,

05:01:16.906 --> 05:01:20.213
I need to go slower on this road. They work better if there's a police

05:01:20.713 --> 05:01:24.083
officer right behind them. Yeah, that's true too. Some of them

05:01:24.372 --> 05:01:28.049
actually flash red and blue, but I guess in Utah that's illegal. But those ones

05:01:28.868 --> 05:01:32.625
get you noticing, right? Optical speed bars.

05:01:32.721 --> 05:01:36.158
This is relatively a newer application.

05:01:36.897 --> 05:01:39.779
UDOT's started using these on more curvy roads,

05:01:39.891 --> 05:01:43.128
but what they do is again they slim the lane down.

05:01:43.192 --> 05:01:46.509
They also alert you to the fact there's a curve ahead, and that gets you

05:01:47.009 --> 05:01:50.119
slowing down. And so we have a few places we'd suggest this.

05:01:50.683 --> 05:01:53.970
Pavement markings, just basically the speed limit

05:01:54.470 --> 05:01:57.537
in the road. Something I don't see as much in Utah,

05:01:58.778 --> 05:02:04.164
but— Edge striping. This is the one I was talking about on Ida—

05:02:04.310 --> 05:02:07.828
Or Ira Hodges. Yes, sorry. I used to know all

05:02:07.876 --> 05:02:10.308
the streets everywhere, and now I'm starting to get old.

05:02:11.930 --> 05:02:15.255
But anyway, narrowing the perceived width of the road down

05:02:15.287 --> 05:02:18.905
just with striping actually gets people to slow down.

05:02:19.630 --> 05:02:22.683
Curve warning signage is good, and then flexible delineator

05:02:22.731 --> 05:02:26.528
posts. This is showing the speed limit sign in the middle of an island.

05:02:27.513 --> 05:02:30.420
It's a A cheap way to do an island in this case.

05:02:31.239 --> 05:02:35.273
Obviously, in fact, you've got them on Major

05:02:35.773 --> 05:02:38.992
Street where you've got the pedestrian crossings and you've got

05:02:39.492 --> 05:02:42.553
these islands with the paint in the road. That's an inexpensive way to do it.

05:02:42.650 --> 05:02:46.165
And they are noticeable. Obviously, if you have curbing, that even

05:02:46.181 --> 05:02:47.589
gets people's attention even more.

05:02:49.870 --> 05:02:53.848
And this is just a diagram talking about a summary of each

05:02:54.348 --> 05:02:56.489
one of those that I talked about. I'm not going to go through each one

05:02:56.989 --> 05:03:00.219
unless you guys want me to. It's in your report.

05:03:01.180 --> 05:03:04.571
OK, so no traffic calming needed or recommended.

05:03:05.088 --> 05:03:08.431
These are those areas that we collected data where

05:03:08.544 --> 05:03:12.681
the speeds were under 5 miles an

05:03:13.181 --> 05:03:13.413
hour or less.

05:03:16.268 --> 05:03:19.366
Within 5 miles an hour of the recommended speed limit. Right, right.

05:03:19.628 --> 05:03:23.666
So, um, and so, so our recommendation is these speeds seem

05:03:24.179 --> 05:03:27.352
appropriate for those streets. Um, we didn't

05:03:27.368 --> 05:03:31.358
have crash history that really supported that, that there were needs on

05:03:31.390 --> 05:03:35.124
those streets. So, um, these are the tables just showing each one

05:03:35.172 --> 05:03:38.585
of those locations. Um, and so you'll notice the posted

05:03:39.085 --> 05:03:42.528
speed limit— NP means there is no posted speed limit on that street.

05:03:42.688 --> 05:03:46.586
We assume it's probably 25 in most locations. But we did not find any

05:03:46.618 --> 05:03:50.315
signs on those roads. And then the 85th percentile,

05:03:50.331 --> 05:03:53.802
so again, that's that speed with the majority of people that

05:03:53.834 --> 05:03:57.129
you would call your non-reckless

05:03:57.242 --> 05:04:01.056
drivers are driving. And so as you can see,

05:04:01.441 --> 05:04:05.201
in the case of all of those, they're within that 5-mile-an-hour range.

05:04:05.780 --> 05:04:08.977
And then the crash history, these are speed-related crashes

05:04:09.475 --> 05:04:12.765
on those roads. And so again, The highest one

05:04:12.781 --> 05:04:16.489
was on Aviator Avenue. And again, that is

05:04:16.970 --> 05:04:20.341
an arterial road, I believe, in the city.

05:04:20.598 --> 05:04:23.069
I think it might be a collector. Right. It has a lot of traffic on

05:04:23.569 --> 05:04:26.408
it. So you would expect a few more crashes on that road.

05:04:27.563 --> 05:04:29.602
Any questions on these ones before I move on?

05:04:31.014 --> 05:04:34.288
Okay. Okay. 11 locations with

05:04:34.788 --> 05:04:38.302
moderate needs. These are the ones, again, that 5 to 10 miles over the posted

05:04:38.802 --> 05:04:42.554
speed limit. We put crash

05:04:43.054 --> 05:04:45.478
history may support adjustments, and I'll go over those here in a minute.

05:04:46.795 --> 05:04:50.619
The proposals that we have for mitigation

05:04:50.989 --> 05:04:54.331
projects are about $400,000 for these roads.

05:04:55.488 --> 05:04:59.103
And again, in the next month, we'll— we have one other element

05:04:59.603 --> 05:05:01.663
of this study we're still going to work on now that we've gotten through this

05:05:02.163 --> 05:05:05.296
part. We do get to write you up a nice study that'll have all the

05:05:05.796 --> 05:05:09.954
details of all of this. So let's see, Mustang Way,

05:05:10.356 --> 05:05:14.269
we're proposing radar feedback signs and landscaping.

05:05:14.720 --> 05:05:18.554
Now, I'll say landscaping on that road would help a ton, 'cause it's

05:05:19.054 --> 05:05:22.585
just straight as can be. But you also have your limitations that

05:05:22.908 --> 05:05:25.439
you don't own that land, right? Part of it's in HOA.

05:05:25.826 --> 05:05:29.774
So The hard part with that road

05:05:30.274 --> 05:05:34.362
